[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays Be7 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/2efb330d-987a-437c-8c89-6181a75d83f7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. And we bring our bishop out to f4. This is the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. e3 is part of our ideas in this opening. The point is to get a c3/d4/e3 structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black opens up their dark-squared bishop. We develop our knight. We haven't been forced to play c3 just yet.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After black undermines our d4 pawn... We support our central structure by adding another protector with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black brings their knight out... And so will we. Notice how we control the e5 square very well. This is another idea in the London.} 6.Ngf3 6...Be7 {Black places their bishop on a very natural square and prepares castling. We'll put our bishop on d3 where it stares at h7.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we'll advance our knight into e5.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Taking the knight immediately is popular but probably not a good idea. Just remember to recapture with the pawn.} 9.dxe5 9...Nd7 {Retreating to d7, to keep an eye on the e5 pawn. We will begin a pawn storm with h4!} 10.h4 10...Qc7 {10...Qc7 is natural to attack the e5-pawn. However, we have a great sacrifice on h7. The engine is able to hold off checkmate, but your opponents aren't engines!} 11.Bxh7+ 11...Kxh7 {Black has to take. We will give a queen check on h5 to force the king back.} 12.Qh5+ 12...Kg8 {The king goes to g8. It has no choice.} 13.Nf3 {We have a massive kingside attack that will likely result in checkmate. Guess what? If 13...f6, you can still play Ng5!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays Be7 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/2efb330d-987a-437c-8c89-6181a75d83f7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. And we bring our bishop out to f4. This is the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. e3 is part of our ideas in this opening. The point is to get a c3/d4/e3 structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black opens up their dark-squared bishop. We develop our knight. We haven't been forced to play c3 just yet.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After black undermines our d4 pawn... We support our central structure by adding another protector with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black brings their knight out... And so will we. Notice how we control the e5 square very well. This is another idea in the London.} 6.Ngf3 6...Be7 {Black places their bishop on a very natural square and prepares castling. We'll put our bishop on d3 where it stares at h7.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we'll advance our knight into e5.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Taking the knight immediately is popular but probably not a good idea. Just remember to recapture with the pawn.} 9.dxe5 9...Nd7 {Retreating to d7, to keep an eye on the e5 pawn. We will begin a pawn storm with h4!} 10.h4 10...f6 {f6 is highly popular here and it must be considered. Additionally, it's not as bad as playing some random move like 10...Re8. But it's still bad. We will go on the attack with Qh5, hitting h7!} 11.Qh5 11...f5 {Black could have played that in one move! Now we can play g4 right away, to great effect.} 12.g4 12...g6 {Black may try to put pressure on our queen. But we can open the position before they can harass us further.} 13.Qh6 13...Kh8 {Fear could be the reason black plays moves like this. We can now break through.} 14.gxf5 14...exf5 {Recapturing.} 15.Nf3 {And that's all, folks. If checkmate is avoided, it will only be with Black giving up material, and even then the attack doesn't stop!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays Be7 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/2efb330d-987a-437c-8c89-6181a75d83f7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. And we bring our bishop out to f4. This is the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. e3 is part of our ideas in this opening. The point is to get a c3/d4/e3 structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black opens up their dark-squared bishop. We develop our knight. We haven't been forced to play c3 just yet.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After black undermines our d4 pawn... We support our central structure by adding another protector with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black brings their knight out... And so will we. Notice how we control the e5 square very well. This is another idea in the London.} 6.Ngf3 6...Be7 {Black places their bishop on a very natural square and prepares castling. We'll put our bishop on d3 where it stares at h7.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we'll advance our knight into e5.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Taking the knight immediately is popular but probably not a good idea. Just remember to recapture with the pawn.} 9.dxe5 9...Nd7 {Retreating to d7, to keep an eye on the e5 pawn. We will begin a pawn storm with h4!} 10.h4 10...f6 {f6 is highly popular here and it must be considered. Additionally, it's not as bad as playing some random move like 10...Re8. But it's still bad. We will go on the attack with Qh5, hitting h7!} 11.Qh5 11...g6 {g6 is a common blunder. We will simply sacrifice on g6, opening up the king's defenses.} 12.Bxg6 12...hxg6 {Black captures. We'll take, giving check.} 13.Qxg6+ 13...Kh8 {Black moves away. We'll force the king back to the g-file.} 14.Qh6+ 14...Kg8 {Only move.} 15.Rh3 {And this gives our rook a chance to give check on the g-file. Black is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays Be7 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/2efb330d-987a-437c-8c89-6181a75d83f7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. And we bring our bishop out to f4. This is the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. e3 is part of our ideas in this opening. The point is to get a c3/d4/e3 structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black opens up their dark-squared bishop. We develop our knight. We haven't been forced to play c3 just yet.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After black undermines our d4 pawn... We support our central structure by adding another protector with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black brings their knight out... And so will we. Notice how we control the e5 square very well. This is another idea in the London.} 6.Ngf3 6...Be7 {Black places their bishop on a very natural square and prepares castling. We'll put our bishop on d3 where it stares at h7.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we'll advance our knight into e5.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Taking the knight immediately is popular but probably not a good idea. Just remember to recapture with the pawn.} 9.dxe5 9...Nd7 {Retreating to d7, to keep an eye on the e5 pawn. We will begin a pawn storm with h4!} 10.h4 10...c4 {Black could try to gain space in the queenside while gaining a tempo on the bishop. But this allows us to play the Greek gift bishop sacrifice!} 11.Bxh7+ 11...Kxh7 {Black must take. Our queen will arrive on h5 with check!} 12.Qh5+ 12...Kg8 {Retreating.} 13.Nf3 {And Ng5 can't be stopped, since even if black goes f6, we can play Ng5 as we'll open up our h-file and get a pawn on g6. Black's king is not surviving for long.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays Be7 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/2efb330d-987a-437c-8c89-6181a75d83f7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. And we bring our bishop out to f4. This is the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. e3 is part of our ideas in this opening. The point is to get a c3/d4/e3 structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black opens up their dark-squared bishop. We develop our knight. We haven't been forced to play c3 just yet.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After black undermines our d4 pawn... We support our central structure by adding another protector with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black brings their knight out... And so will we. Notice how we control the e5 square very well. This is another idea in the London.} 6.Ngf3 6...Be7 {Black places their bishop on a very natural square and prepares castling. We'll put our bishop on d3 where it stares at h7.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we'll advance our knight into e5.} 8.Ne5 8...Nxe5 {Taking the knight immediately is popular but probably not a good idea. Just remember to recapture with the pawn.} 9.dxe5 9...Nd7 {Retreating to d7, to keep an eye on the e5 pawn. We will begin a pawn storm with h4!} 10.h4 10...f5 {f5 is a lot more sound. It blocks the bishop's diagonal, and if we capture en passant Black will be able to bring the knight to f6 to defend the kingside.} 11.Nf3 {But we have Ng5 and g4 ideas to open up the kingside and attack. It's a fairly equal position, but Black will have to defend.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays Bd6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/3553ef18-9053-485c-a418-700aae0c77e7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop our bishop to f4, before playing e3!} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we play e3, consolidating our structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black goes e6 themselves. We'll develop the b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. We'll support our center with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {They develop the queenside knight. And we develop our kingside knight!} 6.Ngf3 6...Bd6 {Now this is the point of the variation: Black proposes a bishop trade. Naturally, we won't comply. Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7.Bg3 {Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7...O-O {A very standard move. Castling is important in every opening. We'll bring our light-squared bishop to d3, eyeing h7.} 8.Bd3 8...Re8 {Black may want to push ...e5 here. But we can stop them by jumping our knight into e5.} 9.Ne5 9...Qc7 {If they take us with 9...Bxe5, 10.dxe5 establishes a powerful e5-pawn that will be supported and defended. Black therefore simply attacks us. We will support the knight by playing f4.} 10.f4 10...cxd4 {With nothing else to do, Black may interject this capture. We will capture with the e-pawn so that they don't get any ...Nb4 moves, harassing our d3-bishop.} 11.exd4 11...Nd7 {This move is a fatal mistake.} 12.Bxh7+ {Check! A Greek Gift! The rook on e8 lacks a defender. Black will lose it if they take our bishop!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays Bd6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/3553ef18-9053-485c-a418-700aae0c77e7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop our bishop to f4, before playing e3!} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we play e3, consolidating our structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black goes e6 themselves. We'll develop the b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. We'll support our center with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {They develop the queenside knight. And we develop our kingside knight!} 6.Ngf3 6...Bd6 {Now this is the point of the variation: Black proposes a bishop trade. Naturally, we won't comply. Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7.Bg3 {Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7...O-O {A very standard move. Castling is important in every opening. We'll bring our light-squared bishop to d3, eyeing h7.} 8.Bd3 8...b6 {b6 defends the c5 pawn. Additionally, it allows the light-squared bishop to develop. We'll break in the center, threatening e5!} 9.e4 9...Be7 {Bringing the bishop back should be played. We can now create some discomfort for Black by pushing forward with e5.} 10.e5 10...Nh5 {The knight should go after the bishop, by all means. Now we have an interesting move. We give up the knight but open up an attack on black's knight and possibly h7.} 11.Ng5 11...Nxg3 {Taking our bishop isn't a good idea... We'll capture on h7 with check.} 12.Bxh7+ 12...Kh8 {And after the king moves... We've got this crafty intermezzo, attacking the queen too.} 13.Nxf7+ {We've got this check, attacking the queen too.} 13...Rxf7 {Black more or less has to capture..} 14.hxg3 {We've got a powerful discovered check coming right up.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays Bd6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/3553ef18-9053-485c-a418-700aae0c77e7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop our bishop to f4, before playing e3!} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we play e3, consolidating our structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black goes e6 themselves. We'll develop the b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. We'll support our center with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {They develop the queenside knight. And we develop our kingside knight!} 6.Ngf3 6...Bd6 {Now this is the point of the variation: Black proposes a bishop trade. Naturally, we won't comply. Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7.Bg3 {Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7...O-O {A very standard move. Castling is important in every opening. We'll bring our light-squared bishop to d3, eyeing h7.} 8.Bd3 8...b6 {b6 defends the c5 pawn. Additionally, it allows the light-squared bishop to develop. We'll break in the center, threatening e5!} 9.e4 9...Be7 {Bringing the bishop back should be played. We can now create some discomfort for Black by pushing forward with e5.} 10.e5 10...Nh5 {The knight should go after the bishop, by all means. Now we have an interesting move. We give up the knight but open up an attack on black's knight and possibly h7.} 11.Ng5 11...Bxg5 {If Black takes our knight (the best move)... We will capture on h5.} 12.Qxh5 12...g6 {Blocking the mate threat on h7. We will retreat the queen to e2 and defend the knight. } 13.Qe2 13...cxd4 {So we're sacrificing a pawn, but... We will attack with h4.} 14.h4 14...Bh6 {The bishop retreats, still with an eye towards our knight. We'll continue our attack, pushing forward with h5.} 15.h5 15...dxc3 {Black can always insert this capture. We will take back.} 16.bxc3 16...Bg7 {Black gets the bishop off the h-file, as we were threatening hxg6, discovering an attack on the bishop. We'll prepare to castle by hand.} 17.Kf1 17...d4 {If d4, trying to open up the position...} 18.c4 {c4 is reliable, keeping the center closed. We also get the e4 square. The position is complicated. We are down a pawn, but the black king faces a strong attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays Bd6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/3553ef18-9053-485c-a418-700aae0c77e7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop our bishop to f4, before playing e3!} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we play e3, consolidating our structure.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black goes e6 themselves. We'll develop the b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Black undermines d4. We'll support our center with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {They develop the queenside knight. And we develop our kingside knight!} 6.Ngf3 6...Bd6 {Now this is the point of the variation: Black proposes a bishop trade. Naturally, we won't comply. Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7.Bg3 {Let's bring the bishop back. If they take, they open up our h-file! 7...Qc7 is a mistake that allows 8.dxc5, and 7...Bxg3 gives us the open h-file! Already two ways Black can go wrong!} 7...O-O {A very standard move. Castling is important in every opening. We'll bring our light-squared bishop to d3, eyeing h7.} 8.Bd3 8...b6 {b6 defends the c5 pawn. Additionally, it allows the light-squared bishop to develop. We'll break in the center, threatening e5!} 9.e4 9...dxe4 {This is the most popular move... but it's not correct. We can just take with the knight, attacking the d6 bishop.} 10.Nxe4 10...Nxe4 {Black alleviates the pressure by trading. We recapture and attack the remaining knight.} 11.Bxe4 11...Bb7 {Black defends the knight. But we take the c-pawn, attacking the d6-bishop.} 12.dxc5 12...Bxc5 {Hence the bishop takes, but...} 13.Qa4 {We gain a tempo on the knight! Many things to consider here. 13...Qe8 or 13...Qc8 allow the 14.Bxh7+ Greek Gift! Maybe best is 13...Na5, when 14.Rd1 Qc8 15.Bd3 follows. Our queen can swing over to h4. Finally, the defense with 13...Rc8 allows us 14.Rd1. Black has to be wary of attacks on their bishops.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays Bd6 Earlier / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/976ac86d-c085-41dd-9bda-089b1be69ca0/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us, and we bring our bishop out!} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll continue with e3.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black plays e6, opening up the dark-squared bishop. We bring our b1-knight out.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Black goes c5. And we support our central d4 pawn.} 5.c3 5...Bd6 {Offering to trade bishops. We reject it. We only want to trade on our terms.} 6.Bg3 6...O-O {Black castles right away. We'll play Ngf3 to control e5.} 7.Ngf3 7...Qc7 {And now Black increases pressure on e5. We can bring the rook to x-ray the queen.} 8.Rc1 8...Nbd7 {Developing the knight and controlling e5 with another piece. We're going to open up the c-file for our rook! With the queen on c7, the idea behind our setup is to create some discomfort for Black.} 9.c4 9...b6 {Black may support the c5-pawn with ...b6. However, this doesn't get rid of the x-ray along the c-file.} 10.b4 {Again, we're opening up the c-file for our rook. Pawn tension on this file benefits us while the queen remains on the c-file. We have ideas of cxd5 to create a pin. The bishop will come out to e2, since on d3 it may be attacked by a knight after trades on c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 4: Black's Other Options / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/bb9ceea6-4e30-4bff-833d-1a57202528d9/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black goes Nf6, standard stuff. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black plays e6, supporting their center. We develop our b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Now Black goes for an early c5. We'll support our central pawn. } 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops. We do likewise. Now we will explore the details associated with not developing the f8-bishop so quickly. Black can go after our bishop with ...Nh5 ideas.} 6.Ngf3 6...Nh5 {Attacking the bishop right away is an option. Let's attack the queen. We want to provoke some weaknesses before potentially trading the bishop off.} 7.Bg5 7...f6 {f6 blocking and attacking our bishop. We'll step back to h4.} 8.Bh4 8...g6 {Supporting the knight. We'll develop the bishop. After Black has gone for ...Nh5 and ...g6, it seems our bishop bites on granite on g6. Thus Black's setup, while not intuitive, makes some sense in that it makes our bishops less effective.} 9.Bd3 9...Be7 {Black unpins their f-pawn. Even though the kingside pawns have been pushed, this engine-approved variation for Black is safe enough because there isn't an immediate way to exploit the pawns on the 6th rank. We'll castle and further our development.} 10.O-O 10...b6 {Black usually goes for a fianchetto. And White, like in many London positions, can orchestrate e4.} 11.Re1 11...O-O {Black castles kingside. Like I said, even though the pawns have been pushed to the 6th rank, the king is safe.} 12.e4 {And now e4. We should be doing alright here. We haven't gotten a winning advantage by any means, but both sides should enjoy having achieved what they wanted. We have ideas of exd5 to open up our rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 4: Black's Other Options / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/bb9ceea6-4e30-4bff-833d-1a57202528d9/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black goes Nf6, standard stuff. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black plays e6, supporting their center. We develop our b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Now Black goes for an early c5. We'll support our central pawn. } 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops. We do likewise. Now we will explore the details associated with not developing the f8-bishop so quickly. Black can go after our bishop with ...Nh5 ideas.} 6.Ngf3 6...cxd4 {Black can insert this capture. We will recapture with the e-pawn, retaining control over the b4 square.} 7.exd4 7...Nh5 {And now Black gains a tempo, attacking our bishop. We will drop it back to e3.} 8.Be3 8...Bd6 {Black finally develops the f8-bishop. We'll play Ne5, a discovered attack on the h5-knight.} 9.Ne5 9...g6 {Black defends the knight. We will retreat because our knight didn't have enough support on e5. Nevertheless, we're still aiming to maintain control over e5.} 10.Nd3 10...O-O {Black castles. Now g3 is a nice move to castle and control f4 and also enable Bg2.} 11.g3 11...f5 {If Black should go wrong, we'll change plans of course. This looks very natural but it allows us to gain multiple tempi on the Black pieces while improving our own pieces. Firstly, we'll attack the f8-rook.} 12.Bh6 12...Re8 {Getting out of the way. We'll block the e-file and attack the knight with Be2.} 13.Be2 13...Nf6 {The knight goes back to f6.} 14.Nf3 {And we can gain control of e5 quite nicely! We also have plans of h4 and h5 if the knight ever moves from f6, and, of course, we're getting a knight on e5 ourselves.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black's Best Setup (d5, e6, Nf6) / Study 4: Black's Other Options / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9b5f5234-fce8-4e63-b23b-dd97db6e3752/studies/bb9ceea6-4e30-4bff-833d-1a57202528d9/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black goes Nf6, standard stuff. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...e6 {Black plays e6, supporting their center. We develop our b1-knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {Now Black goes for an early c5. We'll support our central pawn. } 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops. We do likewise. Now we will explore the details associated with not developing the f8-bishop so quickly. Black can go after our bishop with ...Nh5 ideas.} 6.Ngf3 6...cxd4 {Black can insert this capture. We will recapture with the e-pawn, retaining control over the b4 square.} 7.exd4 7...Bd6 {Developing the bishop now...} 8.Bg3 {Allows us to simply retreat and keep control over e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's c5 Setup (without e6) / Study 1: Black Plays d5, c5, Nf6, Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/3d29484c-7383-49cc-be16-59afe019537c/studies/5da1c4c2-1889-441b-ac47-cc45bf67d2bd/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. Of course we will play c3 and support it.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We continue in a standard way by developing our knight. After ...c5 and ...Nc6, we want to go for queenside development because we want to be able to answer ...Qb6. Moves like 5. Nf3 would be inaccurate.} 5.Nd2 5...Qb6 {Targeting our b-pawn. We'll block the attack, proposing a queen trade to open up the a-file.} 6.Qb3 6...c4 {Black wants to pressure us into trading on b6. Of course, we don't want to activate their rook, so we just go back to c2, preparing to undermine the c4-pawn by playing e4.} 7.Qc2 7...g6 {Black will fianchetto. ...Bf5, supported by the g6-pawn, is another idea. But we can go for the e4 break. This move is good because it will end up weakening the c4-pawn.} 8.e4 8...Nxe4 {Black could take. We'll simply recapture.} 9.Nxe4 9...Bf5 {And if Black wants to be tricky...} 10.f3 {It's not going to go well for them. Black will have to take on e4, and we'll recapture with a strong pawn in the center, plus the fact that c4 will be weak.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's c5 Setup (without e6) / Study 1: Black Plays d5, c5, Nf6, Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/3d29484c-7383-49cc-be16-59afe019537c/studies/5da1c4c2-1889-441b-ac47-cc45bf67d2bd/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. Of course we will play c3 and support it.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We continue in a standard way by developing our knight. After ...c5 and ...Nc6, we want to go for queenside development because we want to be able to answer ...Qb6. Moves like 5. Nf3 would be inaccurate.} 5.Nd2 5...Qb6 {Targeting our b-pawn. We'll block the attack, proposing a queen trade to open up the a-file.} 6.Qb3 6...c4 {Black wants to pressure us into trading on b6. Of course, we don't want to activate their rook, so we just go back to c2, preparing to undermine the c4-pawn by playing e4.} 7.Qc2 7...Bg4 {The bishop is developed with the idea it may make its way to g6. We'll target the c4-pawn immediately by playing b3.} 8.b3 8...cxb3 {Black takes. We recapture and open up our rook.} 9.axb3 9...e6 {And after e6...} 10.b4 {We expand on the queenside! We'll continue with standard piece development (Bd3, Ngf3), but we also have b5 to remove the knight from c6 and get our knight to e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's c5 Setup (without e6) / Study 1: Black Plays d5, c5, Nf6, Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/3d29484c-7383-49cc-be16-59afe019537c/studies/5da1c4c2-1889-441b-ac47-cc45bf67d2bd/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. Of course we will play c3 and support it.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We continue in a standard way by developing our knight. After ...c5 and ...Nc6, we want to go for queenside development because we want to be able to answer ...Qb6. Moves like 5. Nf3 would be inaccurate.} 5.Nd2 5...cxd4 {An alternative is exchanging pawns. We'll recapture with the e-pawn as we would like to retain control over b4.} 6.exd4 6...Bf5 {The option of developing the bishop is always present. In reply, we'll develop.} 7.Ngf3 7...e6 {Black prepares to develop their dark-squared bishop. We attack the b-pawn.} 8.Qb3 8...Qc8 {Defending. Now we go after the f5-bishop.} 9.Nh4 9...Bg6 {Black retreats. We'll claim the bishop pair.} 10.Nxg6 10...hxg6 {Black recaptures. Even though the h-file opens up, the h2-pawn isn't so weak because of our London bishop. We'll develop our bishop to its best diagonal.} 11.Bd3 11...Be7 {Preparing castling. We'll castle.} 12.O-O 12...O-O {Black castles. We'll transfer the a-rook to the semi-open e-file.} 13.Rae1 13...a6 {Black prepares queenside play.} 14.Qd1 {But we're going to start transferring pieces to the kingside. Then we can even push the pawns in front of our king, given that all our pieces are there!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's c5 Setup (without e6) / Study 1: Black Plays d5, c5, Nf6, Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/3d29484c-7383-49cc-be16-59afe019537c/studies/5da1c4c2-1889-441b-ac47-cc45bf67d2bd/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. Of course we will play c3 and support it.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We continue in a standard way by developing our knight. After ...c5 and ...Nc6, we want to go for queenside development because we want to be able to answer ...Qb6. Moves like 5. Nf3 would be inaccurate.} 5.Nd2 5...cxd4 {An alternative is exchanging pawns. We'll recapture with the e-pawn as we would like to retain control over b4.} 6.exd4 6...Bf5 {The option of developing the bishop is always present. In reply, we'll develop.} 7.Ngf3 7...e6 {Black prepares to develop their dark-squared bishop. We attack the b-pawn.} 8.Qb3 8...Qc8 {Defending. Now we go after the f5-bishop.} 9.Nh4 9...Be4 {Black goes to a square where the bishop is defended by the knight. Now we can simply win the bishop pair.} 10.Nxe4 10...dxe4 {The idea is to take away our f3 square.} 11.g3 {We'll use g2 for our knight, e2 for our bishop, and castle. Then we'll open the position for our bishop pair with f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's c5 Setup (without e6) / Study 1: Black Plays d5, c5, Nf6, Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/3d29484c-7383-49cc-be16-59afe019537c/studies/5da1c4c2-1889-441b-ac47-cc45bf67d2bd/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. Of course we will play c3 and support it.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We continue in a standard way by developing our knight. After ...c5 and ...Nc6, we want to go for queenside development because we want to be able to answer ...Qb6. Moves like 5. Nf3 would be inaccurate.} 5.Nd2 5...cxd4 {An alternative is exchanging pawns. We'll recapture with the e-pawn as we would like to retain control over b4.} 6.exd4 6...Bf5 {The option of developing the bishop is always present. In reply, we'll develop.} 7.Ngf3 7...e6 {Black prepares to develop their dark-squared bishop. We attack the b-pawn.} 8.Qb3 8...Qc8 {Defending. Now we go after the f5-bishop.} 9.Nh4 9...Be4 {Black goes to a square where the bishop is defended by the knight. Now we can simply win the bishop pair.} 10.Nxe4 10...Nxe4 {Black recaptures with the knight.} 11.Nf3 {We just come back. We're going to calmly play Bd3 and 0-0, and claim that Black's queen on c8 doesn't achieve much. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's c5 Setup (without e6) / Study 1: Black Plays d5, c5, Nf6, Nc6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/3d29484c-7383-49cc-be16-59afe019537c/studies/5da1c4c2-1889-441b-ac47-cc45bf67d2bd/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. Of course we will play c3 and support it.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We continue in a standard way by developing our knight. After ...c5 and ...Nc6, we want to go for queenside development because we want to be able to answer ...Qb6. Moves like 5. Nf3 would be inaccurate.} 5.Nd2 5...cxd4 {An alternative is exchanging pawns. We'll recapture with the e-pawn as we would like to retain control over b4.} 6.exd4 6...Bg4 {The bishop can also be developed with a tempo on our queen. But we gain a tempo right back by hitting b7.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {Black defends their b-pawn. We'll continue to develop.} 8.Ngf3 8...e6 {Consolidating the central pawn structure. We will place our bishop on the long diagonal.} 9.Bd3 9...Be7 {Black develops the bishop. And we castle.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Castling is natural, and popular here. But it allows us to gain a tempo on the bishop by playing Ne5.} 11.Ne5 11...Bh5 {If Black gets out of the way...} 12.Ndf3 {We can bring our other knight to the party and we stand slightly better. We'll want to bring our a-rook into the e-file, although rook from f to e1 is also fine. Leaving the rook on f1 is often good in case we go for f4 ideas after moving the pieces away from the f-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 1: The Reverse Albin (e4!?) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/a74ee3fb-3f8c-479c-8a8f-6edb09ad4f2b/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {Black can attack our center at this very early juncture. With our reply of 3.e4, we are essentially playing an Albin countergambit (1. d4 d5 2. c4 c5), but since we are White, we have the extra move Bf4.} 3.e4 3...cxd4 {The other way of capturing. We will recapture with the queen, also attacking Black's d5 pawn.} 4.Qxd4 4...dxe4 {The only logical follow-up - otherwise we will take on d5. Even though we're down a pawn for the moment, the endgame will be fine for us. We're ahead in development and black will find it difficult to keep the pawn.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {Black recaptures the queen. We'll attack the e4 pawn and get ready to castle queenside.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black defends the e4 pawn. Let's castle with check!} 7.O-O-O+ 7...Nbd7 {Black blocks the check and develops a piece at the same time.} 8.Nge2 {Even though queens are off the board, our lead in development and Black's exposed king gives us an advantage. We will soon regain the pawn by playing Ng3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 1: The Reverse Albin (e4!?) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/a74ee3fb-3f8c-479c-8a8f-6edb09ad4f2b/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {Black can attack our center at this very early juncture. With our reply of 3.e4, we are essentially playing an Albin countergambit (1. d4 d5 2. c4 c5), but since we are White, we have the extra move Bf4.} 3.e4 3...dxe4 {Black accepts our gambit, in the style of the Albin. We move our pawn forward, securing a space advantage.} 4.d5 4...Nf6 {Black develops their knight and attacks our important d5 pawn. 5.Nc3 is the only reasonable way of guarding our attacked d5 pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...e6 {It's a mistake when white plays e3 in the Albin in the corresponding position there, and it's a mistake here for the same reason! We will check on b5! When Black blocks the check, they will be unable to recapture on e6 with their bishop.} 6.Bb5+ 6...Bd7 {The most obvious interposition. Even though it looks like the bishop is both blocking the check and defending the e6 pawn, the latter is an illusion. We take on e6, and because of the pin to the king, Black cannot recapture with their bishop.} 7.dxe6 7...fxe6 {The only way Black can recapture, as we noted. We'll trade off bishops.} 8.Bxd7+ 8...Qxd7 {This recapture makes sense. Black is behind in development and proposes a queen trade. We have no way of avoiding the endgame, but actually it is okay for us, in spite of being down a pawn for the moment.} 9.Qxd7+ 9...Nbxd7 {Definitely the best recapture.} 10.O-O-O {Despite being down a pawn, we are better. We are ahead in development and will win back the pawn on e4 soon by bringing our g1 knight to g3, leaving Black with a weakness on e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 1: The Reverse Albin (e4!?) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/a74ee3fb-3f8c-479c-8a8f-6edb09ad4f2b/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {Black can attack our center at this very early juncture. With our reply of 3.e4, we are essentially playing an Albin countergambit (1. d4 d5 2. c4 c5), but since we are White, we have the extra move Bf4.} 3.e4 3...dxe4 {Black accepts our gambit, in the style of the Albin. We move our pawn forward, securing a space advantage.} 4.d5 4...Nf6 {Black develops their knight and attacks our important d5 pawn. 5.Nc3 is the only reasonable way of guarding our attacked d5 pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...a6 {A clever move - Black realizes that a bishop check on b5 would be annoying, so denies us access to that square. We'll get ready to win back the e4 pawn and also to castle queenside.} 6.Qe2 6...g6 {Black gets ready to develop the kingside bishop to g7. We castle, placing our rook on a great square. It guards our d5 pawn and is opposite the Black queen.} 7.O-O-O 7...Bg7 {Black develops their bishop and gets ready to castle. We'll finally recapture the sacrificed pawn.} 8.Nxe4 8...Nxe4 {Black trades off a pair of knights.} 9.Qxe4 {We recapture and have good attacking chances. The open e-file may also be relevant once we connect our rooks. We will definitely consider advancing our h-pawn in the near future.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 1: The Reverse Albin (e4!?) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/a74ee3fb-3f8c-479c-8a8f-6edb09ad4f2b/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {Black can attack our center at this very early juncture. With our reply of 3.e4, we are essentially playing an Albin countergambit (1. d4 d5 2. c4 c5), but since we are White, we have the extra move Bf4.} 3.e4 3...dxe4 {Black accepts our gambit, in the style of the Albin. We move our pawn forward, securing a space advantage.} 4.d5 4...Nf6 {Black develops their knight and attacks our important d5 pawn. 5.Nc3 is the only reasonable way of guarding our attacked d5 pawn.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {If Black realizes that e6 is a mistake, they may instead opt to fianchetto their bishop, just like White often does in the standard Albin countergambit.} 6.Qd2 {We have a very easy attack. When Black plays Bg7, we will be ready to trade it off with Bh6. We can castle queenside next and start an attack by advancing our h pawn, which is often an effective strategy against fianchettos.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {With the idea of playing Bxc5 next, if allowed, so we prevent it by playing b4!} 5.b4 5...a5 {Black should try to challenge our queenside structure immediately. Otherwise, we will consolidate by playing a3 and simply be up a pawn. We protect the pawn. We cannot play a3 instead because of the pin on the a file.} 6.c3 6...axb4 {Capturing is natural. We recapture. Logical, logical, as GM Hikaru would say.} 7.cxb4 7...b6 {This is a standard idea in some similar positions. Black wants to regain the pawn using the pin of the c5 pawn to the b4 pawn. However, it fails here for tactical reasons. We will want to check on b5, but first we remove a defender from Black's queenside.} 8.Bxb8 8...Rxb8 {The rook recaptures.} 9.Bb5+ {This check is very hard to meet. If black blocks the check with a piece on d7, then c6 will win material. After Ke7, we will still play c6 with a crushing position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {This is the most popular move. I suggest we pin the knight.} 5.Bb5 5...Qa5+ {Black could give a check, which in turn is also attacking the bishop! That's why we must block the check and defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...a6 {If we are attacked with a6... Just take the knight.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {When Black recaptures... Our superior development should yield some form of advantage for us.} 8.Nf3 8...e6 {Play could continue with the solid e6. Then castling has the additional benefit of unpinning our knight.} 9.O-O 9...Bd7 {The bishop trapped behind the pawns is bad in these positions. After we play a3, Black must take on c5 while they can!} 10.a3 10...Qxc5 {The queen captures... We will gain a tempo on the queen with the knight.} 11.Na4 11...Qa7 {After the queen retreats...} 12.c4 {Undermining the center works well to keep our advantage! We have ideas of Ne5 and b4 if allowed. If ...dxc4, Ne5 and Nxc4 should be our plan to control the d6-square even further.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {This is the most popular move. I suggest we pin the knight.} 5.Bb5 5...Qa5+ {Black could give a check, which in turn is also attacking the bishop! That's why we must block the check and defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...a6 {If we are attacked with a6... Just take the knight.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {When Black recaptures... Our superior development should yield some form of advantage for us.} 8.Nf3 8...Qxc5 {The queen captures the pawn. We will continue by placing a knight in the center.} 9.Ne5 9...e6 {e6 is a popular move in these positions. We will castle. We stand slightly better because Black's bishop on c8 is not a very good piece.} 10.O-O 10...Bb7 {Attempting to improve the bishop... But we can gain a tempo on the queen.} 11.Na4 11...Qa5 {Black retreats, attacking the knight... But c4 undermines the center and opens up a defense on our knight!} 12.c4 12...Be7 {When Black develops the bishop...} 13.b4 {We can gain another tempo on the queen. The idea is Rb1 if the pawn is taken.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {This is the most popular move. I suggest we pin the knight.} 5.Bb5 5...Qa5+ {Black could give a check, which in turn is also attacking the bishop! That's why we must block the check and defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...a6 {If we are attacked with a6... Just take the knight.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {When Black recaptures... Our superior development should yield some form of advantage for us.} 8.Nf3 8...Ne4 {Attacking c5 and the knight on c3! We will respond with Qd4, defending.} 9.Qd4 9...Nxc3 {Black could liquidate. Let's accept the trades.} 10.Qxc3 10...Qxc3+ {And after a series of trades...} 11.bxc3 11...f6 {Black wants to play e5 and shut our bishop down. We'll play Bc7! It keeps our bishop alive and active even after e5 is played.} 12.Bc7 12...e5 {Black expands with e5...} 13.Bb6 {But we won't have a single bad piece in the resulting endgame. We have Nd2 and c4 to look forward to.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...Qa5+ {This does win back the pawn for Black, but clearly taking on c5 with the queen isn't as good as taking with the bishop would be. We block the check by bringing our knight to d2. We don't play Nc3, because then it would be a target for a ...Ne4 by Black.} 5.Nd2 5...Qxc5 {Black recaptures the pawn on c5. We'll continue developing our pieces.} 6.Ngf3 6...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and acquires some control over the e5 square.} 7.a3 {We are ready to expand on the queenside with b4 and c4 next. We are already ahead in development and Black will have to lose more time moving their queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...Nc6 {This is the most popular move. I suggest we pin the knight.} 5.Bb5 5...Qa5+ {Black could give a check, which in turn is also attacking the bishop! That's why we must block the check and defend the bishop.} 6.Nc3 6...e6 {...e6 this early on is not good. It blocks the bishop in and is not effective in recovering the pawn. We'll play a3, when Bxc5 fails to b4, forking queen and bishop.} 7.a3 7...Ne4 {Complicating matters might be a good solution in certain scenarios...} 8.Ne2 {But not in this one. If Nxc3 we can just recapture with our knight and everything is defended. b4 is a threat, so capturing the c5-pawn won't be easy.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {With the idea of playing Bxc5 next, if allowed, so we prevent it by playing b4!} 5.b4 5...a5 {Black should try to challenge our queenside structure immediately. Otherwise, we will consolidate by playing a3 and simply be up a pawn. We protect the pawn. We cannot play a3 instead because of the pin on the a file.} 6.c3 6...Nc6 {Putting more pressure on our queenside - we have to do something about the attack on the b4 pawn. We'll develop, and deal with the attack on our b4 pawn by pinning the knight.} 7.Bb5 7...Be7 {Again, when you see this alternative move, just realize it's too slow. We'll just develop too, while holding on to the pawn.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Castling. Let's take on c6. This prevents b6 from ever happening.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {Black now has a new c-pawn, and b6 undermining our structure will never happen. Our knight can now hop into the center and attack c6.} 10.Nd4 10...Qd7 {Black defends the pawn.} 11.O-O {And we castle. We'll have a nice game holding on to the extra pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {With the idea of playing Bxc5 next, if allowed, so we prevent it by playing b4!} 5.b4 5...a5 {Black should try to challenge our queenside structure immediately. Otherwise, we will consolidate by playing a3 and simply be up a pawn. We protect the pawn. We cannot play a3 instead because of the pin on the a file.} 6.c3 6...Nc6 {Putting more pressure on our queenside - we have to do something about the attack on the b4 pawn. We'll develop, and deal with the attack on our b4 pawn by pinning the knight.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {So Black unpins. We'll protect b4 once again. The game is still complicated, but we are up a pawn and not in any immediate danger.} 8.Qb3 8...Ne4 {Black places the knight on an active square. We kick it out right away.} 9.f3 9...e5 {Black plays actively and complicates matters; after all, they are a pawn down! But this trade is favorable. } 10.fxe4 10...exf4 {Black takes. We'll take on d5, with a huge center. Black will have to play actively and creatively to stay in the game.} 11.exd5 11...Qh4+ {And if Black checks us.} 12.Kd1 {We will move to d1. The most challenging move to meet will be ...Qf2, when we need to find Ne2, with a complex position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {With the idea of playing Bxc5 next, if allowed, so we prevent it by playing b4!} 5.b4 5...a5 {Black should try to challenge our queenside structure immediately. Otherwise, we will consolidate by playing a3 and simply be up a pawn. We protect the pawn. We cannot play a3 instead because of the pin on the a file.} 6.c3 6...Nc6 {Putting more pressure on our queenside - we have to do something about the attack on the b4 pawn. We'll develop, and deal with the attack on our b4 pawn by pinning the knight.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {So Black unpins. We'll protect b4 once again. The game is still complicated, but we are up a pawn and not in any immediate danger.} 8.Qb3 8...Nh5 {Black can go for the bishop pair. We'll get ready to capture with a piece, by playing 9.Ne2.} 9.Ne2 9...Nxf4 {Black takes, and we'll recapture with the knight.} 10.Nxf4 10...g6 {Black gets ready to fianchetto.} 11.a4 {We keep a strong queenside. Remember to go Ra2 after Bg7.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {With the idea of playing Bxc5 next, if allowed, so we prevent it by playing b4!} 5.b4 5...a5 {Black should try to challenge our queenside structure immediately. Otherwise, we will consolidate by playing a3 and simply be up a pawn. We protect the pawn. We cannot play a3 instead because of the pin on the a file.} 6.c3 6...Nc6 {Putting more pressure on our queenside - we have to do something about the attack on the b4 pawn. We'll develop, and deal with the attack on our b4 pawn by pinning the knight.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {So Black unpins. We'll protect b4 once again. The game is still complicated, but we are up a pawn and not in any immediate danger.} 8.Qb3 8...axb4 {If axb4...} 9.cxb4 {Just recapture.} 9...Ne4 {Black clears f6.} 10.Ne2 {But we prepare to block with Nbc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 2: We Play 4.dxc5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/2e6e92ac-845d-43be-8f81-2ec58e7586c2/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...c5 {It's very common for Black to make this pawn break in the London. They can try it here, or even as early as the previous move. While Black will get some compensation, we can actually take this pawn. It isn't easy for them to recover the pawn with their bishop.} 4.dxc5 4...e6 {With the idea of playing Bxc5 next, if allowed, so we prevent it by playing b4!} 5.b4 5...Be7 {This, for example, is too slow. We can connect all our pawns here.} 6.a3 6...O-O {Black castles.} 7.Nd2 {And we've got normal development, plus we have preserved the extra pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 3: The Stonewall Setup (6. Bd3) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/3005e1dc-232f-464f-a703-94d04d5f6b43/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 {We develop the bishop.} 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll reply with e3.} 3.e3 3...e6 {And Black plays e6. We'll reply by developing our knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After c5 undermining our structure... We'll support our center with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We'll bring our king's bishop to the active post on d3.} 6.Bd3 6...Bd6 {And when Black proposes the trade of bishops... We will, unusually for the London system, accept.} 7.Bxd6 7...Qxd6 {Black recaptures. And boom! We enter our Stonewall by playing f4. We're going for total control of e5, where we'll eventually jump a knight into.} 8.f4 8...b6 {...b6 is often played to develop the bishop. We'll bring our other knight to f3.} 9.Ngf3 9...Bb7 {A very logical continuation. b6 was played to fianchetto, after all. We will leap our knight into e5!} 10.Ne5 10...O-O {Black castles. We start attacking with g4!} 11.g4 11...Ne7 {Black defends their light squares.} 12.Qf3 {Our queen is joining the attack! We can go for attacking ideas such as g5 and Qh3. The knight on f6 can't jump into e4 because we just take the material and consolidate the pawn up advantage if that happens.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Bonus Weapon vs ...c5 / Study 3: The Stonewall Setup (6. Bd3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/8d178f9e-f2d7-4ddf-bdb8-f9d5bbda9cfc/studies/3005e1dc-232f-464f-a703-94d04d5f6b43/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll develop the bishop.} 2.Bf4 {We develop the bishop.} 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll reply with e3.} 3.e3 3...e6 {And Black plays e6. We'll reply by developing our knight.} 4.Nd2 4...c5 {After c5 undermining our structure... We'll support our center with c3.} 5.c3 5...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. We'll bring our king's bishop to the active post on d3.} 6.Bd3 6...Bd6 {And when Black proposes the trade of bishops... We will, unusually for the London system, accept.} 7.Bxd6 7...Qxd6 {Black recaptures. And boom! We enter our Stonewall by playing f4. We're going for total control of e5, where we'll eventually jump a knight into.} 8.f4 8...cxd4 {Black can insert this capture to weaken our pawn grip. We'll take back on d4 with the c-pawn, otherwise we would lose the pawn on f4. The c-file is now open.} 9.cxd4 9...Bd7 {Developing the bishop. We'll play a3 to prevent the Black knight from jumping into b4.} 10.a3 10...O-O {Black castles. Our knight has to come to f3 in these structures.} 11.Ngf3 11...Ne7 {A standard defensive maneuver. We'll castle.} 12.O-O 12...Bc6 {The bishop goes to c6 now that the knight has vacated that square. And now our knight reaches e5!} 13.Ne5 13...Nd7 {Black challenges our knight on e5. We attack h7 and threaten mate. An idea can be the rook lift with Rf3-h3!} 14.Qh5 14...f5 {Black could try to block the dangerous diagonal. We'll exchange on d7, forcing one of Black's well-placed pieces to retreat and capture.} 15.Nxd7 15...Qxd7 {The queen takes. Now we plan to get our remaining knight to the e5 outpost.} 16.Nf3 16...Qe8 {Black offers a queen trade to ease the pressure. In some positions, we would decline, but here we are leaving Black with markedly inferior minor pieces, thus the ending is very promising for us.} 17.Qxe8 17...Rfxe8 {Black captures.} 18.Rfc1 {And we're first on the c-file! Our knight will also be very powerful on e5! Black's bishop looks like a big pawn! We can go for doubling rooks on the c-file, and we will make use of the fact that we're quicker than Black in reaching key squares with our pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bf5 {Bf5 is a logical possibility. Black develops their light-squared bishop. However, this move does undefend b7. We'll develop the g1-knight.} 6.Ngf3 6...Qb6 {Applying pressure on b2. We'll grab a pawn, attacking the queen.} 7.dxc5 7...Qxb2 {Grabbing our b-pawn. We'll play Nd4, attacking bishop and knight. If ...Nxd4, we recapture with the c-pawn and our pawn structure is not only fixed, but extremely solid.} 8.Nd4 8...Bg6 {Black retreats. We will bring our bishop out, pinning the c6-knight.} 9.Bb5 9...Rc8 {The most played move. However, it allows us to simply castle and place greater pressure on Black's position. For example...} 10.O-O 10...a6 {If Black attempts to force matters... We will take on c6 and put pressure on the weak queenside pawns.} 11.Bxc6+ 11...bxc6 {Black recaptures, and now they have weaknesses on a6 and c6.} 12.Qa4 {And we can even enter piece sacrifice territory. If ...Qxd2, Qxa6 attacks the rook and puts pressure on the c6-pawn, which would fall with check. It's hard to find a safe square for the rook!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bf5 {Bf5 is a logical possibility. Black develops their light-squared bishop. However, this move does undefend b7. We'll develop the g1-knight.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Connecting the pawns. Now Qb3 attacks the b7-pawn and we don't mind if Black defends with their queen. We can use our pressure to transform the position in our favor.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {This is the most solid, defending b7. We'll play Nh4. Our idea is to get rid of the light-squared bishop, which is often a piece Black likes holding on to when they develop it to f5.} 8.Nh4 8...Be4 {Going towards the center. We'll push the bishop back. We would rather capture on g6 than on e4, when dxe4 would leave our h4-knight looking awkward.} 9.f3 9...Bg6 {Black may have wanted to provoke f3. We'll capture.} 10.Nxg6 10...hxg6 {Black recaptures.} 11.g4 {This expansion is promising. Our bishops are coming back to g2 and g3 and we'll push our kingside pawns to open up the position for them. We've also got kingside attack ideas!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bf5 {Bf5 is a logical possibility. Black develops their light-squared bishop. However, this move does undefend b7. We'll develop the g1-knight.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Connecting the pawns. Now Qb3 attacks the b7-pawn and we don't mind if Black defends with their queen. We can use our pressure to transform the position in our favor.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {This is the most solid, defending b7. We'll play Nh4. Our idea is to get rid of the light-squared bishop, which is often a piece Black likes holding on to when they develop it to f5.} 8.Nh4 8...Bg6 {Black may want to recapture with their h-pawn to open up their rook. We'll take anyway.} 9.Nxg6 9...hxg6 {Black recaptures and opens up the h-file.} 10.h3 {We neutralize the h-file and also create a retreat square for our bishop. Our bishop will be developed to e2 and we'll play 0-0. We can gain space with a4 and placing rooks on the c- and d-files is always a good plan.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bf5 {Bf5 is a logical possibility. Black develops their light-squared bishop. However, this move does undefend b7. We'll develop the g1-knight.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Connecting the pawns. Now Qb3 attacks the b7-pawn and we don't mind if Black defends with their queen. We can use our pressure to transform the position in our favor.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {This is the most solid, defending b7. We'll play Nh4. Our idea is to get rid of the light-squared bishop, which is often a piece Black likes holding on to when they develop it to f5.} 8.Nh4 8...Bg4 {Black saves the bishop for a while longer. We'll chase it with our pawns.} 9.h3 9...Bh5 {Retreating. With g4, we can make it flee even farther.} 10.g4 10...Bg6 {Now it's just back where it should've gone, and we will take it with our knight.} 11.Nxg6 11...hxg6 {The h-file is opened for the rook. We had better not fall for any tricks with Nxg4!} 12.Bg2 {We fianchetto. The bishop holds the kingside together. It will be unopposed, so we will have the bishop pair advantage. We'll of course castle kingside and then place rooks on the c- and d-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bf5 {Bf5 is a logical possibility. Black develops their light-squared bishop. However, this move does undefend b7. We'll develop the g1-knight.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Connecting the pawns. Now Qb3 attacks the b7-pawn and we don't mind if Black defends with their queen. We can use our pressure to transform the position in our favor.} 7.Qb3 7...Qb6 {A very popular yet inaccurate move. This allows us a rather forcing continuation that gives us an easier to play position. We'll begin by exchanging queens.} 8.Qxb6 8...axb6 {The pawns are doubled. We'll pin the c6-knight and threaten Ne5.} 9.Bb5 9...Nd7 {Black has to prevent Ne5. Of course, they may just allow it if they play something natural like ...Be7.} 10.O-O {We just castle. The pawns on b6 and b7 are doubled. We've got h3 ideas to preserve our strong bishop on the diagonal, and it's hard to think of a scenario in which Black will challenge our London Bishop. Also at any point, a4 is a useful move to defend our bishop on b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bf5 {Bf5 is a logical possibility. Black develops their light-squared bishop. However, this move does undefend b7. We'll develop the g1-knight.} 6.Ngf3 6...Qb6 {Applying pressure on b2. We'll grab a pawn, attacking the queen.} 7.dxc5 7...Qxb2 {Grabbing our b-pawn. We'll play Nd4, attacking bishop and knight. If ...Nxd4, we recapture with the c-pawn and our pawn structure is not only fixed, but extremely solid.} 8.Nd4 8...Ne4 {This move is good. It puts pressure on d2. Our queen is overloaded. It cannot defend the rook on a1 and the knight on d2 at the same time. Thus, we'll play Qc1, offering a trade of queens.} 9.Qc1 9...Qxc1+ {Black accepts.} 10.Rxc1 {And we recapture. The position is fairly equal. We have plans of Bb5 and 0-0, but it really depends on what Black chooses to do. If they trade knights with ...Nxd2, we'll have to recapture with the king and lose castling rights. If ...e5, we can always capture the bishop if it's still on f5 or the knight on c6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bg4 {Black develops their bishop with a tempo, hitting our queen. We'll develop the g1-knight and block the threat.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Black consolidates their structure. But we can set up a trap by pinning the c6-knight with Qa4. One idea is Ne5. The bonus trap is 7.Qa4 Bd6 8.Ba6! and the tactics work in our favor.} 7.Qa4 7...Nd7 {Black blocks the pin and may think of transferring the knight to b6. We stop that idea by adding another attacker.} 8.Bb5 8...Qb6 {Black defends c6. We'll jump into e5, attacking all the knights and even the bishop on g4.} 9.Ne5 9...Ndxe5 {If Black gets rid of the pressure... This allows this jump attacking all the knights and even the bishop on g4.} 10.dxc5 10...Bxc5 {If Black captures with the bishop...} 11.Bxe5 {We recapture, opening up an attack on the bishop and attacking the g7 pawn. Black will lose material and we have nothing to worry about, just consolidating.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bg4 {Black develops their bishop with a tempo, hitting our queen. We'll develop the g1-knight and block the threat.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Black consolidates their structure. But we can set up a trap by pinning the c6-knight with Qa4. One idea is Ne5. The bonus trap is 7.Qa4 Bd6 8.Ba6! and the tactics work in our favor.} 7.Qa4 7...Nd7 {Black blocks the pin and may think of transferring the knight to b6. We stop that idea by adding another attacker.} 8.Bb5 8...Qb6 {Black defends c6. We'll jump into e5, attacking all the knights and even the bishop on g4.} 9.Ne5 9...Ndxe5 {If Black gets rid of the pressure... This allows this jump attacking all the knights and even the bishop on g4.} 10.dxc5 10...Nd3+ {Black gives this check as a distraction and as a more favorable way of returning the piece. We've got to capture.} 11.Bxd3 11...Bxc5 {Recapturing the pawn on c5..} 12.b4 {And we've got a great queenside initiative! Achieving b4 with a tempo is good for White, since b5 will follow, dislodging the knight from c6. Further on, we have plans of Be5 to attack the g7-pawn, and 0-0. c4 is a pawn break that should be contemplated in the future.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...c5 and 5...Bf5 or 5...Bg4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/0536fd73-f46d-49ac-9d70-1840c4558824/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...c5 {Black undermines our center. We support it with c3.} 4.c3 4...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll continue in standard fashion with Nd2.} 5.Nd2 5...Bg4 {Black develops their bishop with a tempo, hitting our queen. We'll develop the g1-knight and block the threat.} 6.Ngf3 6...e6 {Black consolidates their structure. But we can set up a trap by pinning the c6-knight with Qa4. One idea is Ne5. The bonus trap is 7.Qa4 Bd6 8.Ba6! and the tactics work in our favor.} 7.Qa4 7...Nd7 {Black blocks the pin and may think of transferring the knight to b6. We stop that idea by adding another attacker.} 8.Bb5 8...Qb6 {Black defends c6. We'll jump into e5, attacking all the knights and even the bishop on g4.} 9.Ne5 9...Ndxe5 {If Black gets rid of the pressure... This allows this jump attacking all the knights and even the bishop on g4.} 10.dxc5 10...Qxc5 {This option is just bad. We'll take on e5, uncovering an attack on the g4-bishop.} 11.Bxe5 11...Bf5 {Black should save their bishop. But this allows us more attacking choices. We'll play our bishop to d4.} 12.Bd4 12...Qd6 {The queen must go to a safe square, ideally to d6 because ...Qe7, although safe, blocks in the f8-bishop. But we can play e4 and take advantage of the queen's position. At first sight, it looks like we're losing a pawn with this move.} 13.e4 {But we can take advantage of the queen's position. It looks like we're losing a pawn with this move.} 13...dxe4 {However, this pawn isn't free.} 14.Nc4 {This is an attack on the queen. We're practically winning. For example, ...Qc7, and we can castle queenside (0-0-0). We've got pressure along the d-file and Be5 is even on the horizon.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bg4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/1bd98380-6d77-4d7a-ad9b-13f00f53495f/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We'll reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {In this chapter, we will consider what happens when black plays ...Bg4 instead of Bf5. Fortunately, our regular London move Nf3 also blocks the attack to our queen.} 4.Nf3 4...e6 {Getting ready to develop the dark-squared bishop and castle. We will attempt to punish black's early ...Bg4 by hunting down this bishop.} 5.h3 5...Bh5 {Black retreats.} 6.g4 {We'll play g4, breaking the pin.} 6...Bg6 {The only way for Black to save the bishop.} 7.Ne5 {We have an idea of playing h4-h5, trapping the g6 bishop, which is awkward to guard against. We have a nice position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bg4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/1bd98380-6d77-4d7a-ad9b-13f00f53495f/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Brings the g8 knight to the most natural square and starts developing the kingside. We'll reinforce our d4 pawn and get ready to develop our other bishop in the future.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {In this chapter, we will consider what happens when black plays ...Bg4 instead of Bf5. Fortunately, our regular London move Nf3 also blocks the attack to our queen.} 4.Nf3 4...e6 {Getting ready to develop the dark-squared bishop and castle. We will attempt to punish black's early ...Bg4 by hunting down this bishop.} 5.h3 5...Bxf3 {This capture will not benefit black.} 6.Qxf3 {Now we've got the bishop pair and we'll have an unopposed light-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...Bg4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/1bd98380-6d77-4d7a-ad9b-13f00f53495f/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. This is one of Black's two most common responses. We bring our bishop to a great square. This is the start of the London.} 2.Bf4 2...Bg4 {It is also possible to play ...Bg4 on move two, but it makes less sense when it's not attacking anything yet. We start our chase of black's bishop by playing f3. We will gain a lot of time on it and have a space advantage on the kingside.} 3.f3 3...Bh5 {Black retreats the bishop.} 4.Nc3 {We can play for e4 in the future, but g4 and h4-h5 is also a serious idea when we gain time on black's bishop and threaten to trap it. This will give us a nice space advantage on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...Qb6 {Black interposes with their queen. We'll play c5, attacking the queen.} 8.c5 8...Qxb3 {Black trades. We'll recapture, opening up our rook.} 9.axb3 9...a6 {A defense against the pawn advance to b5. We'll still push forward.} 10.b4 10...Rc8 {Black escapes the pawn push. The rook move unpins their a-pawn. We'll play h3, creating a square for the f4-bishop to go back to.} 11.h3 11...Be7 {Black develops.} 12.Nd2 {We're headed to a5. We've got a whole lot more space, and we're going to attack black's weak points! Our dark-squared bishop is a nuisance in the position that cannot be traded!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {Black defends from c8. But our rook is ultimately coming to c1. First, we will open the c-file by capturing on d5.} 8.cxd5 8...exd5 {Black takes in the center with the pawn. With Rc1, we place the queen under immediate pressure.} 9.Rc1 9...Be7 {Developing the bishop. Our knight can leap into b5!} 10.Nb5 10...O-O {Black gets out of the threatened fork on c7. But we can still throw our bishop into d6, attacking the Black counterpart!} 11.Bd6 11...Qd8 {Black defends their bishop and unpins the c-pawn.} 12.Qa3 {But we add another attacker! People will most surely take with ...Bxd6, after which Nxd6 attacks b7 and the f5-bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {Black defends from c8. But our rook is ultimately coming to c1. First, we will open the c-file by capturing on d5.} 8.cxd5 8...exd5 {Black takes in the center with the pawn. With Rc1, we place the queen under immediate pressure.} 9.Rc1 9...a6 {Adding another guard against the b5 jump.} 10.Ne5 {We jump into the center and we enjoy more space. At least we don't have a queen on c8! Be2 and 0-0 are next, and if ...Nxe5, we recapture with the d-pawn, removing the knight from f6. At that point, there'll be c-file pin tactics!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc8 {Black defends from c8. But our rook is ultimately coming to c1. First, we will open the c-file by capturing on d5.} 8.cxd5 8...Nxd5 {Recapturing with the knight is also possible.} 9.Bg3 {You can consider this a win. Black now has one less central pawn! Rc1 and Be2 are sensible continuations. There's pressure along the c-file that Black will have to deal with.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {Black can elect to pin us. But we can give a check, forcing the Black knight to block the c-pawn.} 6.Qa4+ 6...Nc6 {The knight blocks the check and defends the bishop. We proceed with development.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Castling. We'll active our rook and support c3.} 8.Rc1 8...dxc4 {Black takes. We'll recapture.} 9.Bxc4 9...Nd5 {Adding pressure to c3. We'll force black's hand by capturing on d5.} 10.Bxd5 10...exd5 {The pawn recaptures.} 11.O-O {And castling should be better for us. Black has blocked their own c-pawn. We've got ideas of retreating the queen to pressure the b-file. In addition, the knight can jump to a4 and later to c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {Black can elect to pin us. But we can give a check, forcing the Black knight to block the c-pawn.} 6.Qa4+ 6...Nc6 {The knight blocks the check and defends the bishop. We proceed with development.} 7.Nf3 7...Ne4 {Adding an attacker on the knight.} 8.Rc1 {We just add a defender.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...b6 {Black pushes their pawn but this leaves weaknesses. We can simply recapture. Our bishop gains a nice diagonal, and c6 can become tender.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {This move really opens up the queenside light squares a lot more, especially the entry point on b5. Black is lost. We play Nh4 and our light square control will be brutal.} 9.Nh4 9...Bg6 {Trying to recapture with the h-pawn.} 10.Nb5 {But the absence of the c-pawn allows our knight in. White is winning. If ...Rc8, we first insert Nxg6 and then follow it up by taking on a7 with the knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...b6 {Black pushes their pawn but this leaves weaknesses. We can simply recapture. Our bishop gains a nice diagonal, and c6 can become tender.} 8.cxd5 8...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight and we will trade, eliminating the attacker of f4 prior to invading with our light-squared bishop.} 9.Nxd5 9...exd5 {Black recaptures.} 10.Ba6 {And now our bishop infiltrates. We're already threatening Bb7 to win the exchange, which is impossible to stop.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...b6 {Black pushes their pawn but this leaves weaknesses. We can simply recapture. Our bishop gains a nice diagonal, and c6 can become tender.} 8.cxd5 8...exd5 {Black recaptures away with the e-pawn.} 9.Ba6 {We're infiltrating. The a8-rook is in trouble.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 3: Black Plays 3...Bf5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/7f40636a-326a-4525-90f9-0f8fb653b0da/lines"]

1.d4 {We open with d4.} 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll bring the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black brings the knight out. We play e3 now that the bishop is out.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And black has developed their light-squared bishop, preparing e6. We can begin undermining the center with c4. The early ...Bf5 can be exploited by going for a Queen's Gambit-like approach.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their center. But we bring the knight to pressure the center. Because of c4, they'll have a hard time defending d5.} 5.Nc3 5...c6 {c6, simply defending the central pawn, is another option. It's ultra-solid, but the problem is the knight usually wants to go to c6. So this already is a bit passive. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...Nbd7 {Black develops theirs. We'll bring the queen out and attack the b-pawn.} 7.Qb3 7...Qb6 {Black interposes with their queen. We'll play c5, attacking the queen.} 8.c5 8...Qxb3 {Black trades. We'll recapture, opening up our rook.} 9.axb3 9...Be7 {The idea is pure development. But we'll push forward with the b-pawn, pressurizing the Black position!} 10.b4 10...O-O {It was too late for a6 either way. With b5, we now underminine the entirety of the structure and get rid of the doubled pawn.} 11.b5 11...cxb5 {Black takes.} 12.Bxb5 {And we develop the bishop with a better position. We've also got a-file doubling to come.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...e6 {Supporting the pawn with e6 is the most popular. We'll increase the pressure in the center.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {Black can choose to develop their knight. We'll just continue with e3.} 5.e3 5...Nf6 {Developing the knight. We'll deploy our rook on the c-file.} 6.Rc1 6...Be7 {Developing the bishop. We'll develop our king's knight.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Castling.} 8.a3 {And we get ready to expand on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...dxc4 {Black can continue with this natural reaction. We'll develop our knight.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3, opening up our bishop.} 5.e3 5...e6 {Black continues with their development. And we recover the pawn.} 6.Bxc4 6...Bb4 {Now pinning our knight is popular. But we can ignore the pin and develop.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Castling. And we attack the bishop with Qb3.} 8.Qb3 8...Nc6 {Black defends. And we will castle.} 9.O-O 9...Bxc3 {If Black ever captures... We just recapture with the queen.} 10.Qxc3 10...Nd5 {If Black forks us...} 11.Bxd5 {We can just eliminate that knight. We've got better queenside prospects than black!} 11...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. Our queen stands better placed on the semi-open c-file! You don't want to take on c7 right away. Rather, just increase c-file control with a move like Rfc1. The knight on c6 will be glued to that square to avoid dropping c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...e6 {Supporting the pawn with e6 is the most popular. We'll increase the pressure in the center.} 4.Nc3 4...Bb4 {Black thinks pinning us is a good idea. But we can just capture the d-pawn.} 5.cxd5 5...Bxc3+ {Eliminating the knight might be a try. Naturally, we will recapture.} 6.bxc3 6...exd5 {Black captures with the pawn. We'll play the calm e3.} 7.e3 7...Nf6 {Black develops. Our knight has a good future on g3.} 8.Ne2 8...O-O {Castling is natural.} 9.h4 {And now we can expand with h4. We're playing Ng3 next!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...e6 {Supporting the pawn with e6 is the most popular. We'll increase the pressure in the center.} 4.Nc3 4...Bb4 {Black thinks pinning us is a good idea. But we can just capture the d-pawn.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. We'll attack the bishop with Qb3.} 6.Qb3 6...Bxc3+ {The bishop takes our knight. We'll recapture with the queen, attacking c7.} 7.Qxc3 7...c6 {Moving the attacked pawn. We'll shift our queen over to g3, putting pressure on the dark squares and hitting g7.} 8.Qg3 8...Nf6 {Developing and offering us the g-pawn. We prefer to focus on the dark squares rather than potentially getting our queen into trouble with the black rook.} 9.Bc7 9...Qe7 {The queen moves away. We'll attack her again. Black was trying to be tricky and get ...Qb4+ in.} 10.Bd6 10...Qd8 {The queen stays defending.} 11.f3 {But now f3 protects our central squares. It seems Black will have a tough time castling, too. We've also got Qe5+ ideas if Nh5 attacks our queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...e6 {Supporting the pawn with e6 is the most popular. We'll increase the pressure in the center.} 4.Nc3 4...Bb4 {Black thinks pinning us is a good idea. But we can just capture the d-pawn.} 5.cxd5 5...Bxc3+ {Eliminating the knight might be a try. Naturally, we will recapture.} 6.bxc3 6...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. We'll prepare to expand in the center by playing f3. We threaten a pawn fork with e4.} 7.f3 7...Bg6 {Stepping out of danger. But we'll expand anyway.} 8.e4 8...Qc6 {The queen retreats, and hits c3.} 9.Qb3 {Threatening to win the queen with Bb5.} 9...a6 {And we can stop here. We've got a huge center and the bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...e6 {Supporting the pawn with e6 is the most popular. We'll increase the pressure in the center.} 4.Nc3 4...Bb4 {Black thinks pinning us is a good idea. But we can just capture the d-pawn.} 5.cxd5 5...Qxd5 {Black takes with the queen because our knight is pinned. We'll play f3. This move, threatening e4, is dangerous in many of these positions.} 6.f3 6...Bg6 {Getting out of the fork. We'll expand anyway.} 7.e4 7...Qd7 {The queen backs off. We'll pose a question to the pinning bishop by playing a3.} 8.a3 8...Ba5 {They choose to keep the pin.} 9.Bc4 {That's ok. We'll just develop our pieces around our huge center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Black Plays Bf5 or Bg4 / Study 4: The Copycat London: 2...Bf5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/da2d34a1-a8f5-43da-a944-e429718e1305/studies/9ad325c7-04dc-41ad-a7a9-15b46b364ec7/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We get the bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Bf5 {And lo and behold! Black does the same! Ok! Now here we can deviate and Black can't continue copying us. With 3.c4 we're attacking the d5 pawn.} 3.c4 3...dxc4 {Black can continue with this natural reaction. We'll develop our knight.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll play e3, opening up our bishop.} 5.e3 5...e6 {Black continues with their development. And we recover the pawn.} 6.Bxc4 6...Bd6 {...Bd6 offering a trade is another popular move. We'll decline. This pin comes with the tricky idea of capturing on f6.} 7.Bg5 7...h6 {Black plays h6 to attack us... We'll capture on f6.} 8.Bxf6 8...gxf6 {Black can't recapture with the queen, as e4 and e5 wins material. In the resulting position, we've got a better pawn structure and d5 will be played next! Dangerous stuff for Black!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 1: The Noob Setup: d5, Nf6, Nc6?! / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/7af8e7e4-9722-49a5-a7e6-7f138b30864f/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll enter the London!} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {Black blocks their c-pawn. We'll play our standard e3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {And Black gets the other knight out. We undermine the center with c4! With Black's c-pawn immobile, undermining d5 becomes a whole lot more effective. That's why we're going for c4 instead of the usual London-style setup.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their central strongpoint. We'll develop our knight, pressurizing d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Bd6 {This move is also quite popular, proposing a trade of bishops. We'll accept, because we believe we have the better light-squared bishop.} 6.Bxd6 6...Qxd6 {Black recaptures. And we will develop, controlling the center.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Castling.} 8.c5 {We attack the queen and place another pawn on the dark squares. Going for a pawn on the same color complex is a great idea after trading off the bishop of that color complex.} 8...Qe7 {And the queen retreats. We've got a pretty good dark-squared control. Attacking the c6 knight is an idea. You'll notice black's plan of e5 is harder to do since d5 lacks support.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 1: The Noob Setup: d5, Nf6, Nc6?! / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/7af8e7e4-9722-49a5-a7e6-7f138b30864f/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll enter the London!} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {Black blocks their c-pawn. We'll play our standard e3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {And Black gets the other knight out. We undermine the center with c4! With Black's c-pawn immobile, undermining d5 becomes a whole lot more effective. That's why we're going for c4 instead of the usual London-style setup.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their central strongpoint. We'll develop our knight, pressurizing d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Be7 {Developing the bishop is also an option. However, it does nothing for the situation in the center. We'll develop our knight.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {They castle. We'll give our bishop gets a retreat square. If Black goes for ...Be7, there's no reason why we shouldn't aim to keep our dark-squared bishop on the long diagonal unopposed.} 7.h3 7...h6 {Black creates luft for their king.} 8.a3 {And we get ready to play on the queenside, where Black is clumsy because of the c6-knight blocking the c-pawn. With our rook coming to the c-file, we'll have a scenario in which there'll be some x-raying pressure on c7. We'll want to delay the light-squared bishop development to give Black some time to play ...dxc4 to let us recapture with the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 1: The Noob Setup: d5, Nf6, Nc6?! / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/7af8e7e4-9722-49a5-a7e6-7f138b30864f/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll enter the London!} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {Black blocks their c-pawn. We'll play our standard e3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {And Black gets the other knight out. We undermine the center with c4! With Black's c-pawn immobile, undermining d5 becomes a whole lot more effective. That's why we're going for c4 instead of the usual London-style setup.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black supports their central strongpoint. We'll develop our knight, pressurizing d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {Black can choose to pin us. We'll protect our knight by bringing our rook to c1.} 6.Rc1 6...O-O {Black castles.} 7.Nf3 {And we develop our knight. We've got plans of Bd3 and castling kingside. Black might continue with Ne4, but the knight on c3 is sufficiently defended. If there are trades on c3, this would only give us another c-pawn that we can use to undermine the Black center.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 2: Reverse Jobava London: d5, Nc6, Bf5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/85b8405d-c2ed-4eb0-a0fa-beb061f6dfbe/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black responds in like manner. We'll get our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {The knight is developed. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And Black plays their bishop out where it eyes our c2 pawn. Let's not fall for ...Nb4 ideas. With c4, we undermine the center! This move is more effective when d5 lacks support. That's why we go for it right away, instead of our usual c3/d4/e3 structure.} 4.c4 4...e6 {...e6 supports the central pawn. We'll develop our queen's knight. 5...Nf6 would be a transposition to the 4...Nf6 line.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {Now a popular move for Black is to pin our knight. We'll capture on d5.} 6.cxd5 {We take in the center.} 6...exd5 {Black recaptures. We'll bring our other knight out to defend c3. We also have Ng3 ideas.} 7.Ne2 7...Nf6 {Black develops. We'll attack the bishop on b4 by playing a3.} 8.a3 8...Be7 {They retreat. Now we'll attack the other bishop with Ng3.} 9.Ng3 9...Be6 {It retreats.} 10.Bd3 {And we bring our bishop out to a safe d3 square where it controls the light squares leading to h7. b4, then b5 is useful in these setups.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 2: Reverse Jobava London: d5, Nc6, Bf5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/85b8405d-c2ed-4eb0-a0fa-beb061f6dfbe/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black responds in like manner. We'll get our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {The knight is developed. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And Black plays their bishop out where it eyes our c2 pawn. Let's not fall for ...Nb4 ideas. With c4, we undermine the center! This move is more effective when d5 lacks support. That's why we go for it right away, instead of our usual c3/d4/e3 structure.} 4.c4 4...Nf6 {Nf6 is another way of supporting d5. We'll develop our knight, pressurizing d5.} 5.Nc3 5...e6 {Black supports their center. We'll prevent the bishop from coming to b4.} 6.a3 6...Bd6 {Black could, for example, continue with ...Bd6. But we'll pin the defender of d5.} 7.Bg5 7...Be7 {Black blocks the pin and thus improves their defense of d5. We'll exchange minor pieces on f6.} 8.Bxf6 8...Bxf6 {Black recaptures. Now we'll force Black's bishop away. We're going to fianchetto and pressure the weak d5 point.} 9.g4 9...Bg6 {Retreating. We'll take on d5.} 10.cxd5 10...exd5 {Recapturing.} 11.Bg2 {And we have developed with tempi and are putting pressure on the black position! Qb3 is on the way, attacking b7 and d5 at the same time.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 2: Reverse Jobava London: d5, Nc6, Bf5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/85b8405d-c2ed-4eb0-a0fa-beb061f6dfbe/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black responds in like manner. We'll get our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {The knight is developed. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And Black plays their bishop out where it eyes our c2 pawn. Let's not fall for ...Nb4 ideas. With c4, we undermine the center! This move is more effective when d5 lacks support. That's why we go for it right away, instead of our usual c3/d4/e3 structure.} 4.c4 4...Nf6 {Nf6 is another way of supporting d5. We'll develop our knight, pressurizing d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Nb4 {This was one of the ideas of Nc6 and Bf5.} 6.Rc1 {But we can simply defend c2 with our rook, with a3 to come. After a3, we still have b4 ideas and we'll continue with our kingside development.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 2: Reverse Jobava London: d5, Nc6, Bf5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/85b8405d-c2ed-4eb0-a0fa-beb061f6dfbe/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black responds in like manner. We'll get our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {The knight is developed. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And Black plays their bishop out where it eyes our c2 pawn. Let's not fall for ...Nb4 ideas. With c4, we undermine the center! This move is more effective when d5 lacks support. That's why we go for it right away, instead of our usual c3/d4/e3 structure.} 4.c4 4...dxc4 {Taking is another popular reaction. This lessens Black's central control. We'll recapture with the bishop, improving our development.} 5.Bxc4 5...e6 {Blocking our bishop's view of f7. We'll develop yet another piece. Our control of e4 is greater now that the pawn isn't on d5.} 6.Nc3 6...Bb4 {Bb4 pinning our knight is now a very common move. But we can develop our king's knight on e2, where it protects our other knight. The pin is ineffective.} 7.Ne2 7...Nf6 {Black brings out their final minor piece.} 8.a3 {Black's ...Bb4 operation only served to give us more time and development and to start our queenside play with a tempo. We'll play b4, Rc1 and 0-0 next, with constant threats of b5 to force the knight off the c6-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 2: Reverse Jobava London: d5, Nc6, Bf5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/85b8405d-c2ed-4eb0-a0fa-beb061f6dfbe/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black responds in like manner. We'll get our bishop out.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {The knight is developed. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...Bf5 {And Black plays their bishop out where it eyes our c2 pawn. Let's not fall for ...Nb4 ideas. With c4, we undermine the center! This move is more effective when d5 lacks support. That's why we go for it right away, instead of our usual c3/d4/e3 structure.} 4.c4 4...dxc4 {Taking is another popular reaction. This lessens Black's central control. We'll recapture with the bishop, improving our development.} 5.Bxc4 5...e6 {Blocking our bishop's view of f7. We'll develop yet another piece. Our control of e4 is greater now that the pawn isn't on d5.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {This sound developing move is also played. We'll also develop.} 7.Nf3 7...Bb4 {People seem to love this pin for some reason. But we can just ignore it and castle.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Black castles, too.} 9.Rc1 {And now our rook enters the party on the c-file! Black has accomplished nothing out of the opening and all our pieces are properly placed. Meanwhile there's a Black knight on c6 which doesn't have any prospects. Qe2 or Qb3 are good ideas to develop the queen, followed by Rfd1.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 3: Black Plays 3...f6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/5ee6175b-f31e-4755-83e3-cf4861f93552/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop as before.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight. We play our natural developing move, connecting our pawns and getting ready to develop our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...f6 {And now f6. The direct idea is e5. Achieving this for black would liberate their position and would justify f6. We'll play Bd3, which controls the g6 square and threatens Qh5+, when the black king may lose castling rights or, if they block with g6, we take with the bishop on d3!} 4.Bd3 4...Be6 {This is the top engine move. It is popular enough that we should consider it. It counters our check on h5 because the bishop would be able to block it with ...Bf7. We'll develop, controlling e5.} 5.Nf3 5...Bf7 {Black relocates the bishop, with the potential to move the e-pawn. Moves like 5...Qd7, could be met with 6. Nc3, and our threat of Nb5 looms over the position. We'll preempt a potential g5, and retreat the bishop.} 6.Bg3 6...e6 {...e6 supporting the center could be played. ...e5 is impossible. We'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Bd6 {Proposing a bishop trade. We'll push c4 and undermine d5!} 8.c4 8...Nge7 {The knight helps support the center.} 9.Nc3 {But we can continue adding pressure. It seems Black's development with f6/Be6/Bf7 has left them with an ineffective setup. We can add pressure with a3 and b4 and with Qc2.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 3: Black Plays 3...f6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/5ee6175b-f31e-4755-83e3-cf4861f93552/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop as before.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight. We play our natural developing move, connecting our pawns and getting ready to develop our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...f6 {And now f6. The direct idea is e5. Achieving this for black would liberate their position and would justify f6. We'll play Bd3, which controls the g6 square and threatens Qh5+, when the black king may lose castling rights or, if they block with g6, we take with the bishop on d3!} 4.Bd3 4...g6 {g6 removes the possibility of checking Black on the h5 square. Let's just develop normally and control the square that Black wanted to expand on.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Black pins us, effectively gaining control of e5 for a potential pawn break next move. How do we lessen Black's control of e5? We create a pin of our own! The knight no longer supports ...e7-e5.} 6.Bb5 6...a6 {Black tries to pressure us into giving up the bishop pair. We'll accept, as this makes e7-e5 harder.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Recapturing. Now we get rid of the pin. If Black gives us the bishop for the knight, then it's a plus. They no longer have the bishop pair advantage. If Black moves away, we unpin our knight and thus control e5 sufficiently.} 8.h3 8...Bf5 {Moving the bishop to safety.} 9.Nc3 {In terms of opening advantage, we've got it, as we've effectively prevented e5 and thus f6 seems odd. Our king will be safer on the kingside, and we can even go Re1 and e4 later to open up the position in our favor.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Black Plays Nc6 Early / Study 3: Black Plays 3...f6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/091c600d-c8af-47f6-85f1-2c63ebbb276c/studies/5ee6175b-f31e-4755-83e3-cf4861f93552/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We develop our bishop as before.} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight. We play our natural developing move, connecting our pawns and getting ready to develop our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...f6 {And now f6. The direct idea is e5. Achieving this for black would liberate their position and would justify f6. We'll play Bd3, which controls the g6 square and threatens Qh5+, when the black king may lose castling rights or, if they block with g6, we take with the bishop on d3!} 4.Bd3 4...g6 {g6 removes the possibility of checking Black on the h5 square. Let's just develop normally and control the square that Black wanted to expand on.} 5.Nf3 5...Bg4 {Black pins us, effectively gaining control of e5 for a potential pawn break next move. How do we lessen Black's control of e5? We create a pin of our own! The knight no longer supports ...e7-e5.} 6.Bb5 6...g5 {Attacking our bishop. We'll go to the only safe square.} 7.Bg3 7...h5 {Threatening to trap our bishop. We'll play h3 and create an escape square while attacking the g4-bishop. Remember, if 8...Bxf3, we benefit from taking one of Black's only developed pieces.} 8.h3 8...Bf5 {Retreating.} 9.c4 {Now we can undermine the center. Black isn't ready for the position to open up against them.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Black's Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Random Moves after 1...d5 (2...c6/g6/f6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9a7ac55d-6093-4f53-84e7-051d48fd0f03/studies/0fdbbea1-6a37-4170-96ac-56b8d37bab65/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll continue with our standard strategy and play Bf4.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {The idea is to fianchetto the bishop. It's not particularly ambitious, though. Let's continue with our standard developing move.} 3.e3 3...Bg7 {Achieving their fianchetto. We'll develop and control e5.} 4.Nf3 4...Nf6 {Black gets ready to castle. We get ready to put our king into safety. Notice we bring our bishop out to e2 whenever there's a pawn on g6. The bishop's scope isn't as good on d3 when the g6-pawn blocks its influence.} 5.Be2 5...O-O {Black castles. And we castle too.} 6.O-O 6...c5 {Black tries to liberate their position and get some counterplay. We stay solid and support our center with c3.} 7.c3 7...Nc6 {Black develops to pressure the center.} 8.h3 {And we create a retreat square for our bishop. As you can see, h3 also takes a square away from Black's bishop, and they have to find a way to develop that piece in the current structure. Meanwhile we'll develop with Nbd2 and our rooks coming to the central files.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Black's Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Random Moves after 1...d5 (2...c6/g6/f6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9a7ac55d-6093-4f53-84e7-051d48fd0f03/studies/0fdbbea1-6a37-4170-96ac-56b8d37bab65/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll continue with our standard strategy and play Bf4.} 2.Bf4 2...f6 {Black opens up their king early. Perhaps they intend e5? e3 is good and if 3...Nc6, we'll transpose to something familiar.} 3.e3 3...e6 {This is a non-losing move that deviates from the d5/Nc6/f6 positions we already know. But here we can still threaten the check on h5!} 4.Bd3 4...Ne7 {The knight adds a defender to the g6 square. Undermining the center is good, plus Black has developed suboptimally, therefore opening the position is to our advantage.} 5.c4 5...c5 {Black chooses to do the same, but this setup with ...f6 is unnatural. The dark-squared bishop is stuck behind the knight. Here we just develop. The idea is that any capture in the center isn't going to solve black's problems or improve their position.} 6.Nc3 6...dxc4 {Capturing away from the center. We'll recapture with the bishop.} 7.Bxc4 7...cxd4 {Another central pawn exchange. With our lead in development, we embrace the isolated queen's pawn. } 8.exd4 8...Nbc6 {Attacking the IQP.} 9.Nge2 {And we defend it! We can agree that e6 is awkward for Black: it blocks their light-squared bishop. We can castle kingside and play Re1, but d5 is also a great move that can destabilize Black's position quickly if they're not careful. d5 and with the bishop on c4 piercing through the diagonal, it'll even be hard for the king to find a safe space.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Black's Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Random Moves after 1...d5 (2...c6/g6/f6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9a7ac55d-6093-4f53-84e7-051d48fd0f03/studies/0fdbbea1-6a37-4170-96ac-56b8d37bab65/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll continue with our standard strategy and play Bf4.} 2.Bf4 2...c6 {And Black plays this passive move, which usually ends up with the bishop behind the pawn. We will support our center, as before, with e3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Now Black develops the knight. Let's undermine their d5 pawn. Our standard development of c4/Nc3 will be effective if Black blocks in their light-squared bishop with e6 at some point.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black will often defend their center. We'll develop our queen's knight, and target d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Be7 {Preparing to castle. Let's develop and control the center.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {Black castles.} 7.Qc2 {And we continue developing, bearing in mind the fact that perhaps Black is delaying the capture on c4 until we develop our f1 bishop. We've got, in the meantime, useful moves to play like h3 and Rc1.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Black's Weird 2nd Moves / Study 1: Random Moves after 1...d5 (2...c6/g6/f6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/9a7ac55d-6093-4f53-84e7-051d48fd0f03/studies/0fdbbea1-6a37-4170-96ac-56b8d37bab65/lines"]

1.d4 1...d5 {Black mirrors us. We'll continue with our standard strategy and play Bf4.} 2.Bf4 2...c6 {And Black plays this passive move, which usually ends up with the bishop behind the pawn. We will support our center, as before, with e3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Now Black develops the knight. Let's undermine their d5 pawn. Our standard development of c4/Nc3 will be effective if Black blocks in their light-squared bishop with e6 at some point.} 4.c4 4...e6 {Black will often defend their center. We'll develop our queen's knight, and target d5.} 5.Nc3 5...Bd6 {Offering a trade of Black's good bishop. We would prefer to exchange on our terms, and not bring the Black queen into the center. Our bishop steps back.} 6.Bg3 6...O-O {Black castles.} 7.Nf3 {And our development continues normally, with Bd3 and e4 to come! After 0-0, we'll bring rooks to d1 and e1 to support our central expansion.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 1: Black Plays 4...b6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/e55cedc5-6b46-4462-acb3-117dc1911797/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {This move is preferred by people who like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicates that ...c5 might be played. With 3.e3, we'll achieve our setup.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermines our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...b6 {This is the Queen's Indian Setup. The dark-squared bishop develops after the ...e6-pawn push and the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed on b7. We'll play Nc3, and eye the chance of attacking the weak c7 and d6 squares with our knight.} 5.Nc3 5...cxd4 {Black has also been known to capture in the center. Nb5 is good here, threatening Nc7+.} 6.Nb5 6...Nd5 {...Nd5 attacks our bishop and defends c7. But again we can take on d4 with our queen.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nc6 {As tempting as ...Nc6 looks...} 8.Qa4 {Our queen can just sidestep the threat and still defend f4. Meanwhile, we're landing something on d6 quite soon. 0-0-0 will also be a powerful move that places a rook on the d-file and pressurizes Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 1: Black Plays 4...b6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/e55cedc5-6b46-4462-acb3-117dc1911797/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {This move is preferred by people who like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicates that ...c5 might be played. With 3.e3, we'll achieve our setup.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermines our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...b6 {This is the Queen's Indian Setup. The dark-squared bishop develops after the ...e6-pawn push and the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed on b7. We'll play Nc3, and eye the chance of attacking the weak c7 and d6 squares with our knight.} 5.Nc3 5...a6 {A sound move for Black is ...a6, preventing our Nb5 jump. But another of the ideas of Nc3 was the d5 push!} 6.d5 6...d6 {Maybe Black wants to consolidate in the center... But we can damage Black's pawns by taking.} 7.dxe6 7...Bxe6 {If they take with the bishop... We've got Ng5!} 8.Ng5 8...Nc6 {Black could desperately seek to continue to develop. If they go back, they just lose a tempo. We'll take on e6.} 9.Nxe6 9...fxe6 {Recapturing.} 10.g4 {And now that the light squares are ours, we can have a party on them! With Bg2, we'll control the long diagonal, specially considering that the light-squared bishop isn't on the board. g5 also threatens to remove the knight from f6: it's hard to suggest a move for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 1: Black Plays 4...b6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/e55cedc5-6b46-4462-acb3-117dc1911797/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {This move is preferred by people who like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicates that ...c5 might be played. With 3.e3, we'll achieve our setup.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermines our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...b6 {This is the Queen's Indian Setup. The dark-squared bishop develops after the ...e6-pawn push and the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed on b7. We'll play Nc3, and eye the chance of attacking the weak c7 and d6 squares with our knight.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb7 {This is the most popular and indeed the most natural continuation: Black played ...b6 to play ...Bb7 after all. But this allows us to jump with our knight and threaten to win material with a fork on c7.} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {Black defends c7. Now Nd2 is rather effective. We're getting the knight to c4 and from there we'll be aiming at d6 with two knights.} 7.Nd2 7...cxd4 {Supposing Black thinks they're off the hook in the center... We'll make them think again with Nc4, focusing on the d6 square.} 8.Nc4 8...Ne4 {Now the knight must jump forward to defend d6. But after exd4, our knights are well placed in the queenside and we have good development. Meanwhile Black has been forced into odd contortions to defend themselves.} 9.exd4 9...d5 {...d5 seems to be the only glimmer of light Black has in their position, driving one of our knights back while supporting their central knight.} 10.Ne3 {But again, our knights are far better placed than the knight on a6 and the bishop on b7.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 1: Black Plays 4...b6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/e55cedc5-6b46-4462-acb3-117dc1911797/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {This move is preferred by people who like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicates that ...c5 might be played. With 3.e3, we'll achieve our setup.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermines our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...b6 {This is the Queen's Indian Setup. The dark-squared bishop develops after the ...e6-pawn push and the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed on b7. We'll play Nc3, and eye the chance of attacking the weak c7 and d6 squares with our knight.} 5.Nc3 5...cxd4 {Black has also been known to capture in the center. Nb5 is good here, threatening Nc7+.} 6.Nb5 6...d6 {...d6 is often a mistake when the queen can somehow join in the attack of d6. Thus we'll capture on d4 with our queen and hit d6.} 7.Qxd4 7...Nd5 {The knight blocks our view of d6, but this allows us a neat tactic. We will make a temporary piece sacrifice with Nd6+!} 8.Nxd6+ 8...Bxd6 {Black captures our knight, and the bishop no longer defends the g7-pawn. We take it and attack the rook.} 9.Qxg7 9...Qf6 {It's important to observe that ...Rf8 fails to Bb5+, which leads to a great loss of material for Black. Thus Black could want to bail out with a queen trade. We'll accept.} 10.Qxf6 {We accept.} 10...Nxf6 {The trade is complete.} 11.Bxd6 {We regain our piece, and we're up material with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 1: Black Plays 4...b6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/e55cedc5-6b46-4462-acb3-117dc1911797/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {This move is preferred by people who like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicates that ...c5 might be played. With 3.e3, we'll achieve our setup.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermines our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...b6 {This is the Queen's Indian Setup. The dark-squared bishop develops after the ...e6-pawn push and the light-squared bishop is fianchettoed on b7. We'll play Nc3, and eye the chance of attacking the weak c7 and d6 squares with our knight.} 5.Nc3 5...cxd4 {Black has also been known to capture in the center. Nb5 is good here, threatening Nc7+.} 6.Nb5 6...d6 {...d6 is often a mistake when the queen can somehow join in the attack of d6. Thus we'll capture on d4 with our queen and hit d6.} 7.Qxd4 7...e5 {Another trick arises when Black thinks they can fork us. But we can take two pawns for our knight.} 8.Nxe5 8...dxe5 {Black captures.} 9.Qxe5+ {And we take with check. Nc7 is coming next. We can stop here as you should win material after all is said and done.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6 threatens our b2 pawn. But just remember to play Na3 and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5.Na3 {But just remember this move and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5...cxd4 {Black can choose to take, thus releasing some tension. We'll insert Nc4. The point is that we attack the queen and defend b2.} 6.Nc4 6...Qb4+ {...Qb4+ is the most common move. We'll block the check, allowing the capture on c3...} 7.c3 7...dxc3 {Black captures, threatening a discovery... But we'll attack the piece with which Black threatens the discovery.} 8.a3 8...c2+ {...c2 is discovered check. But we can take the queen.} 9.axb4 9...cxd1=Q+ {And after Black forces us to take... We'll recapture with the king, and we're threatening the brutal Nb6. The a-pawn is pinned!} 10.Kxd1 10...Nd5 {Nd5 is sometimes played, defending b6 and attacking our bishop. But Nd6+ is quite strong. We're attacking various weak points, thus it must be taken.} 11.Nd6+ 11...Bxd6 {Black takes, and we'll recapture.} 12.Bxd6 12...Nc6 {If Black tries to develop... b5 is an effective move to dislodge the knight.} 13.b5 13...Nce7 {The knight goes back. And we can afford to systematically remove the knights' useful squares.} 14.e4 14...Nf6 {The knight falls back, attacking e4.} 15.Bd3 {But we can play like Karpov, and completely control our opponent's counterplay! We'll continue with moves like Ke2 to connect the rooks and defend the f2-pawn. Rfc1 to attack the c8-bishop is an idea and Black can't even castle yet.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6 threatens our b2 pawn. But just remember to play Na3 and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5.Na3 {But just remember this move and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5...Qxb2 {The move we obviously allowed. Our continuation is rather forcing: we have a way of trapping the queen and activating all our pieces. We'll playing Nb5, threatening a fork on c7 while also cutting off the queen's retreat.} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {Defending c7 from a6. But the queen is running out of squares!} 7.a3 7...Nd5 {Trying to get the knight to assist. We'll attack the queen with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...Qa2 {It gets out of the way...for now. Now we have a sneaky combination. We'll begin with Bd6. The point will become apparent in a couple of moves.} 9.Bd6 9...Bxd6 {A simple trade? You might imagine so. We attack the queen with Bc4.} 10.Bc4 10...Nc3 {If Black captured on c4, Nxd6 was a devastating fork! This move doesn't save Black either. We'll just take the queen.} 11.Bxa2 11...Nxd1 {They capture ours... But the d6-bishop falls with check!} 12.Nxd6+ 12...Ke7 {And after the king moves up to attack our knight...} 13.Nxc8+ {Another piece falls with check and we're taking the knight next! We emerge with an extra piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6 threatens our b2 pawn. But just remember to play Na3 and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5.Na3 {But just remember this move and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5...cxd4 {Black can choose to take, thus releasing some tension. We'll insert Nc4. The point is that we attack the queen and defend b2.} 6.Nc4 6...Qb4+ {...Qb4+ is the most common move. We'll block the check, allowing the capture on c3...} 7.c3 7...dxc3 {Black captures, threatening a discovery... But we'll attack the piece with which Black threatens the discovery.} 8.a3 8...c2+ {...c2 is discovered check. But we can take the queen.} 9.axb4 9...cxd1=Q+ {And after Black forces us to take... We'll recapture with the king, and we're threatening the brutal Nb6. The a-pawn is pinned!} 10.Kxd1 10...Na6 {But Na6 also blocks the pin on the a-pawn. So now the a-pawn could take without losing the rook in the back. You'll notice, however, that we have greater piece activity in this queenless middlegame!} 11.b5 {With b5, obviously, we threaten the knight, and seek to revive the pin we used to have on the a-pawn. White stands better, with a lot of pressure in the position. The only moves for Black to hold are counterattacks like ...d5 and ...Ne4, after which we should just take on a6.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6 threatens our b2 pawn. But just remember to play Na3 and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5.Na3 {But just remember this move and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5...d5 {Straightforward development; also, ...d5 covers c4 against the knight. But it doesn't cover b5!} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {The knight on a6 defends the c7 square. But it's a knight on a6! Not good for Black! We can expand on the queenside with a4!} 7.a4 7...c4 {Black's point is to diminish our defense of the knight. We'll play c3, guarding the b4 square and preparing b3.} 8.c3 8...Bd7 {Attacking our knight. But b3 comes anyway! We undermine the pawn chain!} 9.b3 9...Bxb5 {Black takes. We recapture, obviously.} 10.axb5 10...Qxb5 {It seems Black got a pawn. But after we capture on c4...} 11.bxc4 11...dxc4 {And Black recaptures...} 12.Qb1 {We force a queen trade that will leave us with pressure against all the queenside pawns! After ...Qxb1+, Rxb1 and we're attacking b7 and c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {And now Nc6 puts pressure in the center. We'll bring our knight out.} 5.Nbd2 5...Be7 {Black gets ready to castle. And we create a retreat square on h2 for our bishop.} 6.h3 6...O-O {Castling. And we go for our standard London structure.} 7.c3 7...b6 {Black may want to fianchetto. We'll develop our bishop.} 8.Bd3 8...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. And we castle.} 9.O-O 9...cxd4 {And if this capture ever happens... We just capture with the e-pawn to bring the rook to the file.} 10.exd4 10...d6 {After something like d6... We'll get our bishop out of danger.} 11.Bh2 11...g6 {Black can block our bishop's diagonal. But we'll bring our queen to e2.} 12.Qe2 12...Re8 {The rook opposite the queen prepares a potential ...e5. We'll add more heavy pieces to the e-file.} 13.Rfe1 13...Bf8 {The bishop is redeployed. Black is going to locate it on g7.} 14.a4 {And we expand on the queenside, where we will push our pawns. We have more space: we'll centralize our other rook with Rad1, and we may even play Bb5 to pin the c6-knight, increasing our grip in the position.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {And now Nc6 puts pressure in the center. We'll bring our knight out.} 5.Nbd2 5...Be7 {Black gets ready to castle. And we create a retreat square on h2 for our bishop.} 6.h3 6...O-O {Castling. And we go for our standard London structure.} 7.c3 7...d5 {Placing a pawn in the center. Our bishop arrives to d3.} 8.Bd3 8...b6 {b7 is cleared for the bishop. We'll castle.} 9.O-O 9...Bb7 {And Black fianchettoes the bishop. We'll develop our queen and connect rooks.} 10.Qe2 10...Bd6 {Offering a trade of bishops. We'll ignore it. If ...Bxf4, exf4 our queen and rook are ideally placed.} 11.Rfe1 11...Ne7 {Redeploying the knight. g6 is a useful square to neutralize pressure from our d3-bishop.} 12.Rad1 {All our pieces are centralized. Even though the position is equal, we've got an easy game ahead of us. We'll trade bishops first in order to free our e-pawn and play e4. Besides, we may want to insert dxc5 before e4 to open up our rook along the d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6 threatens our b2 pawn. But just remember to play Na3 and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5.Na3 {But just remember this move and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5...Qxb2 {The move we obviously allowed. Our continuation is rather forcing: we have a way of trapping the queen and activating all our pieces. We'll playing Nb5, threatening a fork on c7 while also cutting off the queen's retreat.} 6.Nb5 6...Nd5 {Defending c7 from d5. We'll attack the queen with Rb1.} 7.Rb1 7...Qxa2 {The queen takes. We'll attack her again.} 8.Ra1 8...Qb2 {She goes back to b2. And now playing our bishop to c4 covers a2. Rb1 threatens to win the queen!} 9.Bc4 9...Qb4+ {Qb4+, attacking our bishop, may be tried.} 10.Nd2 {But we merely retreat, defending the bishop, and we still have Nc7 after Bxd5 ideas! Of course, we'll defend the knight with Rb1 with a tempo first. The queen is still very short of squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nc6 or 4...Qb6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/ea547ad7-ebe4-4c41-8b17-2698d6be5e57/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Now this move is preferred by people that like the Nimzo or Queen's Indian. We'll play our normal London setup.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {...e6, indicating that ...c5 might be played. We'll support our center with e3.} 3.e3 3...c5 {And ...c5 undermining our center. Obviously we won't take, we'll just develop, supporting the pawn.} 4.Nf3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6 threatens our b2 pawn. But just remember to play Na3 and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5.Na3 {But just remember this move and you'll be fine! The knight can come to b5 or c4 and gain a tempo!} 5...Qxb2 {The move we obviously allowed. Our continuation is rather forcing: we have a way of trapping the queen and activating all our pieces. We'll playing Nb5, threatening a fork on c7 while also cutting off the queen's retreat.} 6.Nb5 6...Nd5 {Defending c7 from d5. We'll attack the queen with Rb1.} 7.Rb1 7...Qxa2 {The queen takes. We'll attack her again.} 8.Ra1 8...Qb2 {She goes back to b2. And now playing our bishop to c4 covers a2. Rb1 threatens to win the queen!} 9.Bc4 9...Nc3 {...Nc3, trying to exchange pieces, is another option to try to hold on. But once our knight arrives on c7, the rook on a8 is ours.} 10.Nc7+ 10...Kd8 {Black moves to the side. And we'll propose a queen trade. We can afford to safeguard our queen because the knight on c7 is defended.} 11.Qc1 {And we propose a queen trade. We can afford to safeguard our queen because the knight on c7 is defended.} 11...Qb4 {After the queen threatens a discovery...} 12.Be2 {We've got the calm Be2. And there's no deadly discovery to be seen. We'll be up an exchange pretty soon. If ...Nd5+, we've got c3, forcing trades to give us time to take on a8.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/795202f6-341c-4757-9b45-95215d920358/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll still go for our London.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {Black plays 2...e6. This is very flexible and could still transpose to other main lines. As normal, we'll support our central pawn by playing e3.} 3.e3 3...b6 {And now Black gets ready to fianchetto in a sort of Queen's Indian style. So now I suggest we attack the rook in the corner and develop our queen.} 4.Qf3 4...d5 {...d5 is another move that also blocks the atack. But this move also blocks the bishop's future view along the diagonal on b7. We develop the knight.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb7 {Black develops their bishop. We'll castle queenside.} 6.O-O-O 6...a6 {Black prepares queenside expansion. But we plan to expand on the other side of the board! A future g5 will weaken Black's grip over e4.} 7.g4 7...Nbd7 {Black develops their other knight. We'll press forward with g5.} 8.g5 8...Ne4 {The knight is well defended on e4. Yet we can take and create a weakness on e4.} 9.Nxe4 9...dxe4 {And the bishop on b7 is blocked by the e4 pawn. We'll slide the queen to the side and attack c7.} 10.Qg3 10...Rc8 {And after the rook defends...} 11.Ne2 {We're going to increase pressure on e4. White is already much better! Nc3 and Bg2 will attack e4 and Black still has to get their king to safety at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: The Nimzo & Queen's Indian Setups / Study 3: Black Plays 3...b6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/73ca5eb3-a61e-4f20-9927-349eb13565bb/studies/795202f6-341c-4757-9b45-95215d920358/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll still go for our London.} 2.Bf4 2...e6 {Black plays 2...e6. This is very flexible and could still transpose to other main lines. As normal, we'll support our central pawn by playing e3.} 3.e3 3...b6 {And now Black gets ready to fianchetto in a sort of Queen's Indian style. So now I suggest we attack the rook in the corner and develop our queen.} 4.Qf3 4...Nc6 {Blocking is the most popular move. I suggest we just develop here.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb7 {Black players like to put the bishop opposite the queen. But our argument is that Black doesn't have any good discoveries.} 6.O-O-O 6...Bb4 {The bishop attacks the c3-knight... We'll defend it.} 7.Nge2 7...O-O {Black castles... And we'll go e4 with ideas of e5.} 8.e4 8...d5 {So if Black ever pushes in the center...} 9.e5 {We gain a nice space advantage. We may have ideas of a pawn storm with h4-h5-h6, and we may even have attacking ideas like Rd3 to transfer the rook to the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is officially Pirc territory. Don't worry, we've got a plan for this too. The plan is basically: trade bishops, get the queen to the h-file, trade h-pawns and open up the rook, and give mate on the h-file. Sounds easy, doesn't it? Sometimes it is.} 5.Qd2 5...O-O {Castling is by far the most popular move here. We'll continue with our plan.} 6.O-O-O 6...c6 {Preparing the usual counterplay. We'll offer a trade of bishops by playing Bh6.} 7.Bh6 7...b5 {Black attacks on the queenside. Our next move, f3, safeguards the e4 pawn and guards the g4 square. It removes much of the force behind ...b4 since our center is protected.} 8.f3 8...Nbd7 {Black develops the knight, with the possibility of supporting ...e5 if the time is ever right. However, since we have some neat d-file pressure, it's hard to play ...e5. So, with Black lacking counterplay, we can continue our attack!} 9.h4 9...Qa5 {Black is threatening a two move tactic against our a2-pawn with the pawn push ...b4. A simple sidestep will solve the issue. That's why we defend a2 with the king.} 10.Kb1 10...b4 {...b4 attacking our knight... But we can just bring it back, and have the option of switching it to the kingside.} 11.Nce2 11...Ba6 {After something like ...Ba6 with the idea of activating the bishop... g4 is way too strong: it allows us to play h5 at some point.} 12.g4 12...c5 {This counter-attack is too slow. We'll push ahead with h5.} 13.h5 13...Rfc8 {Black, under great pressure, tries to activate more pieces. Moving the f-rook also gives the king extra room... But our attack is too quick: we open the h-file by capturing on g6.} 14.hxg6 14...fxg6 {Black recaptures. We'll remove the defensive bishop by exchanging on g7, after which our queen will have access to h6.} 15.Bxg7 15...Kxg7 {Once the king recaptures... We've got Qh6+ and we're breaking through!} 16.Qh6+ 16...Kg8 {Black retreats...} 17.Nf4 {And just as an example of how to end a game in the Pirc, we activate yet another piece. Black will have to relinquish material in order to avoid mate.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is officially Pirc territory. Don't worry, we've got a plan for this too. The plan is basically: trade bishops, get the queen to the h-file, trade h-pawns and open up the rook, and give mate on the h-file. Sounds easy, doesn't it? Sometimes it is.} 5.Qd2 5...Nbd7 {Developing the other knight. We'll continue with our plan.} 6.O-O-O 6...O-O {This is the most popular move. But our reply of e5 will cause a lot of problems.} 7.e5 7...Nh5 {Nh5 attacks our bishop and opens up an attack on e5. We'll offer an exchange of bishops and at the same time pin the g7-bishop to the rook.} 8.Bh6 8...dxe5 {Black takes on e5. We recapture. Now Nxe5 loses an exchange due to the pin on the d-file. Bxe5 is a lot more interesting...} 9.dxe5 9...Bxe5 {We can't grab the rook because of Bf4. But we can get our attack going on the kingside since Black is pinned on the d-file. We start a pawn storm with h4!} 10.h4 10...Bd6 {This move is suggested by the engine. It breaks the pin on the knight. But your opponents will have a tough time finding it and they may play some inferior move. We won't let up on the attack: we'll push on with g4.} 11.g4 11...Nhf6 {The knight retreats. I bet black wishes we took that rook!} 12.f3 {Our pieces dominate. Our pawns are optimally placed and h5 is coming. After our king gets to a safer square, we can think about continuing our attack. The knight will come out via h3 and the bishop will go to e2.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is officially Pirc territory. Don't worry, we've got a plan for this too. The plan is basically: trade bishops, get the queen to the h-file, trade h-pawns and open up the rook, and give mate on the h-file. Sounds easy, doesn't it? Sometimes it is.} 5.Qd2 5...Nbd7 {Developing the other knight. We'll continue with our plan.} 6.O-O-O 6...c6 {Black prepares their counterplay on the queenside. We'll again push in the center with e5.} 7.e5 7...dxe5 {Capturing the pawn. We'll recapture with our pawn, opening the d-file.} 8.dxe5 8...Nh5 {Black wants to take our pawn. With ...Nh5, they attack our bishop which in turn defends e5. But we can propose a trade with pressure on the bishop.} 9.Bh6 9...Bxe5 {If Black chooses to take the e-pawn... We'll develop with Nf3, gaining time on the bishop.} 10.Nf3 10...Bg7 {The bishop retreats. We'll begin our pawn storm with g4.} 11.g4 11...Bxh6 {If Black eliminates the tension... We'll recapture.} 12.Qxh6 12...Nhf6 {The knight attacks the g-pawn. But we can develop and target f7, ignoring the pawn on g4.} 13.Bc4 13...Nxg4 {The pawn isn't free, far from it. We'll play Qg7, attacking f7 and the rook on h8 at the same time.} 14.Qg7 14...Rf8 {The rook must defend f7. But our knight jumps into the fray, hitting f7 and h7.} 15.Ng5 15...Nge5 {...Nge5 defends f7 again, but that means it must stay on e5. We play a bold temporary sacrifice on d7. The point is to eliminate the defender of the f8-rook.} 16.Rxd7 16...Bxd7 {So after either the bishop or the queen recapture...} 17.Nxh7 {We capture on h7 and threaten to take on f8 with checkmate. Black is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is officially Pirc territory. Don't worry, we've got a plan for this too. The plan is basically: trade bishops, get the queen to the h-file, trade h-pawns and open up the rook, and give mate on the h-file. Sounds easy, doesn't it? Sometimes it is.} 5.Qd2 5...c6 {A standard move in this position. The idea is to control some central squares, delay castling, and prepare ...b5. Let's trade bishops! This gets rid of an important defender of the dark squares and allows our queen an entry point.} 6.Bh6 6...Bxh6 {Black accepts the trade. And we recapture. Our queen is now an invader in the dark squares.} 7.Qxh6 7...Qa5 {Our knight is pinned, so ...Nxe4 is a threat. We defend e4 with the bishop and develop another piece.} 8.Bd3 8...b5 {After ...b5, our pieces are simply too well placed.} 9.Nf3 9...b4 {...b4 attacks our knight. But our position is solid. We can just move the knight away and nothing bad happens. We come back to e2, and,as the bishop isn't on f1 anymore, our knight doesn't block it.} 10.Ne2 10...Qh5 {Black could beg for a queen trade. They may try going for this because our queen is preventing them from castling.} 11.Qd2 {Take a look at this position. Whose pieces look better? Black has clearly gone astray. e5 is a strong pawn break that's sufficiently supported by our knight on f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...O-O {Believe it or not, thousands of people have played this. The idea is developing the entire kingside and ask questions later. I'm afraid chess doesn't work that way though; you must pay attention to your opponent's threats as well. We'll push e5, displacing the knight!} 5.e5 5...Nh5 {This doesn't place the knight on the back rank. It attacks our bishop, but when we retreat, we are then threatening to win the knight with g4!} 6.Bc1 6...f5 {When will the knight see the light of day again?} 7.f4 {We are in complete control of Black's kingside. The knight is dim on the rim, and the bishop stares at a wall of pawns. We'll develop with g3 and Bg2, Be3 to get the dark-squared bishop out.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...O-O {Believe it or not, thousands of people have played this. The idea is developing the entire kingside and ask questions later. I'm afraid chess doesn't work that way though; you must pay attention to your opponent's threats as well. We'll push e5, displacing the knight!} 5.e5 5...Ne8 {I don't think I've seen a worse knight this early on. It looks like a 960 position.} 6.Qe2 {The point is to castle long and attack on the kingside. h4 and h5 to attack the hook on g6 and open up the h-file for the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is officially Pirc territory. Don't worry, we've got a plan for this too. The plan is basically: trade bishops, get the queen to the h-file, trade h-pawns and open up the rook, and give mate on the h-file. Sounds easy, doesn't it? Sometimes it is.} 5.Qd2 5...c6 {A standard move in this position. The idea is to control some central squares, delay castling, and prepare ...b5. Let's trade bishops! This gets rid of an important defender of the dark squares and allows our queen an entry point.} 6.Bh6 6...Bxh6 {Black accepts the trade. And we recapture. Our queen is now an invader in the dark squares.} 7.Qxh6 7...b5 {Starting the queenside attack! Black often plays this move to initiate their counterplay and some pressure against a2. We'll push forward with e5, and dislodge the knight.} 8.e5 8...dxe5 {Black may try to trade once. But this recapture opens up the d-file. When you don't have a good reason to trade, you shouldn't. And this applies for Black here.} 9.dxe5 9...Nd5 {The knight goes on the open d-file.} 10.O-O-O {So we can pin it to the queen! We've got serious pressure against the knight on d5, and we're threatening just to win a pawn after Nxd5. If ...e6, there's Ne4 intending Nf6+ or Nd6+. The position isn't easy to play for Black, as some quick analysis will show.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 1: London -> Pirc Defense / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/1bdb4622-3017-487e-bc22-ac7e65e3059b/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their standard Nf6. Let's bring the bishop out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Alright, now here Black starts to deviate from what we know. It seems they want to fianchetto. Indeed, this is what happens in the Grunfeld and King's Indian! Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3.Nc3 {Here I recommend the immediate Nc3. I know it blocks our c-pawn. However, we're able to influence the center quicker.} 3...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. This is very natural, as this was the objective of ...g6. And we'll play e4! One of the benefits of Nc3: it supports the e2-e4 push!} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is officially Pirc territory. Don't worry, we've got a plan for this too. The plan is basically: trade bishops, get the queen to the h-file, trade h-pawns and open up the rook, and give mate on the h-file. Sounds easy, doesn't it? Sometimes it is.} 5.Qd2 5...c6 {A standard move in this position. The idea is to control some central squares, delay castling, and prepare ...b5. Let's trade bishops! This gets rid of an important defender of the dark squares and allows our queen an entry point.} 6.Bh6 6...Bxh6 {Black accepts the trade. And we recapture. Our queen is now an invader in the dark squares.} 7.Qxh6 7...b5 {Starting the queenside attack! Black often plays this move to initiate their counterplay and some pressure against a2. We'll push forward with e5, and dislodge the knight.} 8.e5 8...b4 {A counter-attack. Perhaps not a good idea. We can just go back and defend our d4-pawn. The knight on f6 is still under attack.} 9.Nce2 9...Nd5 {The knight goes to the center of the board.} 10.Nf3 {And we've got some pretty dangerous pieces. Meanwhile Black has to deal with our kingside initiative. Because our knight on e2 blocks the bishop, we can fianchetto with g3 and Bg2 followed by 0-0.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...O-O {Black castles. It seems they are not afraid of our h-pawn. But we'll push it forward!} 6.h5 6...c5 {Black goes for counterplay in the center! We'll capture and open the h-file.} 7.hxg6 7...fxg6 {Black opens up the f-file for their rook. We'll play Qd2 in accordance with our plan.} 8.Qd2 8...Nc6 {Putting pressure on our center. We'll reply with Nf3, supporting our center.} 9.Nf3 9...Bf5 {If Bf5 trying to develop another minor piece... We can take on c5.} 10.dxc5 10...Qa5 {Black obviously will try to recover it. But our knight aggressively jumps into the center. If ...Nxd4, we recapture with the pawn and reinforce c5.} 11.Nd4 11...Qxc5 {Supposing the queen just takes... We'll castle queenside and just proceed with our plan.} 12.O-O-O 12...Nxd4 {Should Black think our knight is too annoying on d4... We can recapture with the pawn, and conveniently form a queen and bishop battery!} 13.exd4 13...Qa5 {Getting out of the way and pressurizing a2. But f3 controls the central squares.} 14.f3 14...Rac8 {The rook enters the semi-open c-file. But we'll begin our attack with g4.} 15.g4 15...Bd7 {After the bishop retreats...} 16.Bd3 {We've got many pieces facing the kingside, and our attack will consequently be strong. The queen could stay on d2 to defend. Kb1 is a key defensive move, while Rde1 goes on the offensive to attack e7.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...O-O {Black castles. It seems they are not afraid of our h-pawn. But we'll push it forward!} 6.h5 6...c5 {Black goes for counterplay in the center! We'll capture and open the h-file.} 7.hxg6 7...hxg6 {Capturing towards the center. Our rook has become active on the h-file. Our queen clears the way for castling. We'll have some central pressure, as well as the possibility of connecting rooks.} 8.Qd2 8...Nc6 {Black may develop the knight, putting pressure on d4. We'll develop ours as well.} 9.Nge2 9...Qa5 {The queen usually comes out to a5 to put pressure on the a2-pawn and to get out of the d-file because the rook is coming to the file. We'll play Bh6. The idea is to trade an important defender.} 10.Bh6 10...Bxh6 {Black takes. And we'll recapture.} 11.Rxh6 11...e5 {Black may choose to undermine our center... But the rook falls back to h4, defending the center, while secretly clearing the h6 square for the queen. So you can imagine that after e4 at some point, the queen will be able to land on h6.} 12.Rh4 12...Kg7 {A blunder like this just allows us to accelerate e4... Firstly we exchange pawns on e5.} 13.dxe5 13...Nxe5 {The knight recaptures.} 14.e4 {And we're either winning a pawn in the center or entering h6 with our queen. White is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...h5 {h5 is the most popular move, therefore we will start here. The idea is to stop our pawn advance. We'll just develop another piece.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {Castling is the most popular move. It's a useful developing move and places the king in a safer square. We'll bring our knight to the outpost on e5!} 7.Ne5 7...c5 {Black begins immediate counterplay against our center. We'll play Qd2 to castle queenside.} 8.Qd2 8...Nc6 {Black continues to put pressure in our center. But here I advise taking on c5. Black has a funny way to go wrong.} 9.dxc5 9...Qa5 {Should they try to regain the pawn with the queen... We'll take on c6.} 10.Nxc6 10...bxc6 {Recapturing. And after we play a3...} 11.a3 11...Qxc5 {This is a terrible blunder.} 12.Na4 {The queen is lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...h5 {h5 is the most popular move, therefore we will start here. The idea is to stop our pawn advance. We'll just develop another piece.} 6.Nf3 6...c5 {c5 undermines the center. But this allows us to threaten c7 check.} 7.Nb5 7...Na6 {The knight has to go to the rim in order to defend c7. We'll consolidate our center with c3.} 8.c3 8...O-O {Black castles. Let's just develop. Black is paralyzed on the queenside.} 9.Be2 9...Bd7 {Attacking our knight. We just support it with the a-pawn.} 10.a4 10...c4 {And after c4, disconnecting our bishop from our knight... The simple b3 undermines Black's pawn chain.} 11.b3 11...cxb3 {What else? Black was losing material otherwise... We now recapture with the queen. The paralyzed queenside is now within our aim.} 12.Qxb3 12...Bc6 {Bc6 would be a good defensive move, guarding all the weak points.} 13.O-O {But after castling, we've got plans of Rc1 and c4, and stand better.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...c5 {Immediately undermining the center. We now play Nb5, threatening Nc7. Although we don't often to play it, it makes Black develop in an awkward way with Na6.} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {Defending c7 from an awkward square. We'll continue by developing another piece.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we continue our development. Practically we argue that our pieces are better than Black's because of the a6 knight and the c8 bishop that hasn't found a square yet.} 8.Be2 8...Bd7 {Black places the bishop on d7. We'll support the knight with our pawn.} 9.a4 9...c4 {Disconnecting our knight and bishop. But we can centralize the knight and attack the bishop on d7.} 10.Ne5 10...Bxb5 {Black takes, and we recapture.} 11.axb5 11...Nc7 {Black attacks b5. We'll push forward on the kingside, supported by our bishop. Yes, we're giving up the b5-pawn, but the attack is worth it.} 12.h5 12...Nxb5 {Black takes on b5. And we'll open up the h-file!} 13.hxg6 13...fxg6 {Black captures away from the center and leaves a pawn on h7.} 14.Nxc4 {We're capturing on c4 with check and getting the piece back if Black takes our knight with the d5-pawn. We'll be a pawn up with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...c5 {Immediately undermining the center. We now play Nb5, threatening Nc7. Although we don't often to play it, it makes Black develop in an awkward way with Na6.} 6.Nb5 6...Na6 {Defending c7 from an awkward square. We'll continue by developing another piece.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we continue our development. Practically we argue that our pieces are better than Black's because of the a6 knight and the c8 bishop that hasn't found a square yet.} 8.Be2 8...Bd7 {Black places the bishop on d7. We'll support the knight with our pawn.} 9.a4 9...c4 {Disconnecting our knight and bishop. But we can centralize the knight and attack the bishop on d7.} 10.Ne5 10...Bxb5 {Black takes, and we recapture.} 11.axb5 11...Nc7 {Black attacks b5. We'll push forward on the kingside, supported by our bishop. Yes, we're giving up the b5-pawn, but the attack is worth it.} 12.h5 12...Nxb5 {Black takes on b5. And we'll open up the h-file!} 13.hxg6 13...hxg6 {Capturing towards the center.} 14.c3 {And we've got a plan: Qc2 and taking on g6, opening up everything on the kingside!} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...O-O {Black castles. It seems they are not afraid of our h-pawn. But we'll push it forward!} 6.h5 6...Nxh5 {The knight just takes... We'll sacrifice the exchange to ruin the pawn cover around the black king.} 7.Rxh5 7...gxh5 {Recapturing. What else? Our queen will take on h5 and enter the attack.} 8.Qxh5 8...Nd7 {The best defensive resource, planning to bring the knight to f6.} 9.Bd3 {Yet we're adding many attackers. We have great compensation for the exchange! We'll play Nf3, 0-0-0, and Rh1 at some point to attack the h7-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...h5 {h5 is the most popular move, therefore we will start here. The idea is to stop our pawn advance. We'll just develop another piece.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Black pins our knight, but we're not worried about our pawns being doubled..} 7.Qd2 7...Bxf3 {Black takes, and we recapture.} 8.gxf3 8...c6 {Supporting d5. But now we castle queenside and after e4 we'll have a queen and bishop battery!} 9.O-O-O 9...Nbd7 {Developing the knight... We'll break in the center with e4.} 10.e4 10...e6 {Black sets up a triangle pawn formation. We'll take the precaution of removing our king from the queen's diagonal.} 11.Kb1 11...a6 {Black prepares a queenside pawn storm. We'll put our rook on the semi-open g-file.} 12.Rg1 12...Qe7 {Black develops the queen... But we can immediately exploit the queen's position.} 13.Bg5 13...Qb4 {Should Black try to get out of the pin and place their queen actively...} 14.Bh3 {It seems to me all our pieces are developed, our bishops are putting a lot of pressure on the position, and Black has nothing in terms of an attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Pirc & Grunfeld Setups / Study 2: The Grunfeld Setup / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/d6dcf134-a014-468c-9408-27958cfa2d96/studies/9c29e158-1280-4ec0-b0d4-2e3d99267543/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black begins, intending a hyper-modern approach. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion.} 2.Bf4 2...g6 {Black will fianchetto. Now, seeing how those Pirc positions were so good for us, let's try aiming for that again. We play Nc3.} 3.Nc3 3...d5 {And Black now covers the e4 square with ...d5, and they've entered a Grunfeld setup. We can't play e4, so instead we'll support our center with e3.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. And we can begin launching our kingside attack!} 5.h4 5...c6 {Black can continue with slower play. The problem with this is that they take away c6 from the knight. Let's just develop normally.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Should the knight be pinned... We simply unpin by bringing the bishop to e2.} 7.Be2 7...Nbd7 {Black develops the knight. And we'll get our knight into the center and attack the bishop.} 8.Ne5 8...Bxe2 {A trade of bishops.} 9.Qxe2 {And our queen is left on the e-file where it will support f3/g4 pawn pushes. We're also castling queenside! With our rooks coming to the kingside, we'll be trying to open up this side of the board to attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...d6 {...d6 is the most played move here. It fixes our d-pawn. Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4.Nc3 {Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4...g6 {The idea is to fianchetto. We'll expand in the center.} 5.e4 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll give a check on b5, disrupting Black's coordination. The reasoning is that both queenside minor pieces have to be developed through d7, so when a piece blocks on d7, they'll be cramped.} 6.Bb5+ 6...Bd7 {Black blocks with the bishop and proposes a trade. a4 is a useful move to control the queenside, and the trade isn't bad for us.} 7.a4 7...O-O {Black castles. We'll develop our knight.} 8.Nf3 8...Na6 {Black develops their knight to the only available square. We'll castle, finishing our development.} 9.O-O 9...Nc7 {Rerouting the knight and attacking our bishop. We trade off our worst piece.} 10.Bxd7 10...Nxd7 {Black recaptures with the knight. We'll play Re1, preparing a future e5.} 11.Re1 11...a6 {The idea is b5. So we'll cover the expansion with our queen.} 12.Qd3 12...Rb8 {If Black tries to force the expansion... We cover the b5 push and can respond with en passant, damaging Black's structure.} 13.a5 13...b5 {Just to illustrate, ...b5 wouldn't be a good idea because we can capture en passant.} 14.axb6 14...Rxb6 {Black recaptures with the rook.} 15.Nd1 {We bring the knight back. We've got plans of mounting pressure on the a-pawn. c3 will block off the bishop's diagonal. Nd2-Nc4 may be a plan at the right moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...d6 {...d6 is the most played move here. It fixes our d-pawn. Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4.Nc3 {Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4...e5 {Creating a triangle central pawn structure. Even though this allows an en passant capture, en passant isn't forced. Let's just bring the bishop back.} 5.Bd2 5...Be7 {Developing the bishop is the most popular and actually fairly logical. Mostly, the bishop on e7 prevents a pin on f6 and it doesn't create weaknesses with a g6 fianchetto. We'll expand in the center, with strong pawns.} 6.e4 6...O-O {Black castles. We develop our bishop. Our bishop comes out first because we're controlling the g4-square, preventing ...Bg4.} 7.Be2 7...Nbd7 {Black develops their knight. We will also develop our knight. Our pieces are less cramped. After ...Nbd7, ...Bg4 isn't a threat so we can bring our knight out.} 8.Nf3 8...Ne8 {Black may try to go for a pawn break by inserting the ...Ne8 maneuver. But we can just continue expanding and play a4. Notice how castling isn't a priority at the moment. The center is so closed that we can just continue making minor improvements.} 9.a4 9...g6 {Black's lack of squares for their pieces forces them to create a weakness in the kingside. We attack the rook with our bishop.} 10.Bh6 10...Ng7 {Black blocks, however this implies that there will be a pin on g7. It's not a good sign to have a fianchettoed knight. We decide to maneuver with the f3-knight. The idea is to support h4-h5 with the bishop's influence.} 11.Nd2 11...Kh8 {The king tries to make room for other pieces. Note how cramped Black is.} 12.h4 {But our pawn storm begins, supported by our bishop! h5 is coming, and once we open up the h-file we'll take the knight on g7 forcing the king to recapture. We'll then begin transferring pieces to the kingside to attack the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...d6 {...d6 is the most played move here. It fixes our d-pawn. Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4.Nc3 {Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4...g6 {The idea is to fianchetto. We'll expand in the center.} 5.e4 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll give a check on b5, disrupting Black's coordination. The reasoning is that both queenside minor pieces have to be developed through d7, so when a piece blocks on d7, they'll be cramped.} 6.Bb5+ 6...Nbd7 {Black blocks with the knight. This leaves their minor pieces somewhat uncoordinated. We will play a4, working to make it harder for Black to expand on the queenside with ...a6 and ...b5.} 7.a4 7...O-O {Black castles. We'll develop our knight.} 8.Nf3 8...a6 {Black forces our bishop to retreat.} 9.Be2 {We keep all our pieces on the board and black is cramped. We'll continue with 0-0, Qd2, h3 to keep pieces out of g4 and our position is solid with more space.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...Qb6 {The immediate ...Qb6 attacks our b2 pawn. But we'll just gambit it and develop our queen's knight! We're not afraid of this capture because our development will be quicker than Black's, and the queen will be misplaced.} 4.Nc3 4...d6 {Defending c5, and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll defend the pawn with the queen. If Black doesn't take on b2, we'll defend it. There's no need to give it away if they lose their chance.} 5.Qc1 5...e5 {Placing a pawn in the center and attacking our bishop. We'll attack the knight and threaten to take on f6, wrecking the structure.} 6.Bg5 6...Be7 {Developing and defending. We'll build a strong center with e4.} 7.e4 7...h6 {Wanting to get rid of the bishop... Merely retreating is good.} 8.Bd2 8...Qd8 {The queen had questionable usefulness on b6. We'll check on b5. The point is we will either trade our bad bishop or disrupt black's coordination.} 9.Bb5+ 9...Nbd7 {Black prefers to leave the bishops on. Now a4 is a useful move, expanding on the queenside.} 10.a4 10...O-O {Black castles. And we'll develop our other knight and get ready to castle.} 11.Nge2 11...Ne8 {If Black seeks to prepare kingside counterplay...} 12.Ng3 {Our knight can be transferred to g3 where it has a future on f5. In this position, we have great control over both sides of the board because of our center. We can imagine a future with the bishop back to e2 after something like a6 attacking it, and a kingside pawn storm from us!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...e6 {...e6 yet again undermines our pawn. We can just support it with our knight. We're not worried about ...exd5 as after Nxd5 we're threatening Nc7+.} 4.Nc3 4...d6 {...d6 is a move that needs to be played in order to develop the dark-squared bishop without us attacking it with d6. We add another pawn to support our center.} 5.e4 5...a6 {Black covers b5 and prepares ...b5 themselves. But we can already force a better endgame by opening up the center, starting with the capture on e6.} 6.dxe6 6...Bxe6 {The bishop recaptures. We will push e5, attacking the knight and the pawn. The idea is that we're forcing a pawn trade, exposing the queens along the d-file, which will lead to a trade that's favorable for us.} 7.e5 7...dxe5 {When Black takes our pawn, we can misplace their king by exchanging queens.} 8.Qxd8+ 8...Kxd8 {The king recaptures, but loses castling rights. We'll simply take on e5.} 9.Bxe5 9...Nbd7 {Attacking our bishop.} 10.O-O-O {And we pin the knight to the king. Our coordination is superior in this queenless middle game. When the king leaves the d-file, we can go Bg3 and then develop with Nh3, aiming at the f4-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...b5 {Black plays in the style of the Benko Gambit! We'll play f3. Our idea is to build a strong center.} 4.f3 4...Nh5 {Attacking our bishop... We'll drop our bishop all the way back to c1.} 5.Bc1 5...e6 {...e6 to undermine our pawn. The queen is also unleashed. We develop our knight on h3, where it neutralizes the queen check on h4.} 6.Nh3 6...Bb7 {Attacking d5. We'll play e4, supporting our d-pawn.} 7.e4 7...Qa5+ {If this check ever comes... We'll just block it with our bishop and attack the queen.} 8.Bd2 8...Qb6 {The queen retreats.} 9.c4 {And c4 should consolidate our center. We can expand on the kingside with g4 and face virtually no adverse consequences on the dark squares. Nc3 and Bxc4 if Black takes on c4 are some moves to develop, with 0-0 coming up next.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...Qb6 {The immediate ...Qb6 attacks our b2 pawn. But we'll just gambit it and develop our queen's knight! We're not afraid of this capture because our development will be quicker than Black's, and the queen will be misplaced.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Undermining the pawn with e6 is another alternative. We'll support our center with e4.} 5.e4 5...Nxe4 {Simply taking the pawn that looked free, with the idea of Qb4+, is a possibility as well. We'll capture on e4.} 6.Nxe4 6...Qb4+ {This move forks our pieces. However, there's a way to deal with this which involves giving the knight back. We'll start with Qd2.} 7.Qd2 7...Qxe4+ {Black takes with check. We'll block with the knight, developing yet another piece. So we have bishop, queen and knight developed whereas Black only has their queen out.} 8.Ne2 8...Qxd5 {Black grabs another pawn. But we're so far ahead in development that we can exchange queens and then go on the attack!} 9.Qxd5 9...exd5 {Recapturing, but now Nc3 is very strong.} 10.Nc3 10...d4 {If Black tries to hold on to it...} 11.Nd5 {We're centralizing our knight and threatening to win material! It's borderline impossible to stop this. Test it out for yourself in the analysis!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...Qb6 {The immediate ...Qb6 attacks our b2 pawn. But we'll just gambit it and develop our queen's knight! We're not afraid of this capture because our development will be quicker than Black's, and the queen will be misplaced.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Undermining the pawn with e6 is another alternative. We'll support our center with e4.} 5.e4 5...Qxb2 {The queen could take on b2, attacking the knight.} 6.Nge2 {This accelerates our development. The black queen is badly misplaced. We've got Nb5 ideas to threaten a check on c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...Qb6 {The immediate ...Qb6 attacks our b2 pawn. But we'll just gambit it and develop our queen's knight! We're not afraid of this capture because our development will be quicker than Black's, and the queen will be misplaced.} 4.Nc3 4...Qxb2 {Black takes. Our knight is attacked. But we'll just retreat our bishop to d2, defending the knight. Black is going to have to waste time extricating their queen.} 5.Bd2 5...Qb6 {The queen returns from her trip to b2.} 6.e4 {And we can establish our two central pawns. This idea will also be seen in the Ne4 & c5 Trompowsky!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...d6 {...d6 is the most played move here. It fixes our d-pawn. Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4.Nc3 {Let's develop, supporting our center.} 4...e5 {Creating a triangle central pawn structure. Even though this allows an en passant capture, en passant isn't forced. Let's just bring the bishop back.} 5.Bd2 5...g6 {The point is Black needs to develop the bishop somewhere. On e7, it was blocked in by the d6 and c5 pawns. We support our center. The center is locked, but since we have more space to develop our pieces we'll stand slightly better.} 6.e4 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll fianchetto as well. Since it's a closed position, we need to find a logical square for our light-squared bishop. Playing Bb5+, helping Black trade a piece, doesn't make much sense.} 7.g3 7...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto. Our central pawns are very strong.} 8.Bg2 8...Ne8 {Black intends f5 for counterplay. Unfortunately, this means they're moving the same piece twice in the opening, while the queenside pieces haven't yet moved. We can launch a pawn storm on the kingside, disregarding ...f5.} 9.h4 9...f5 {The intended f5, attacking the base of our center. We don't need to react defensively, however. We can just continue our attack with h5, undermining g6.} 10.h5 10...Nf6 {The knight returns to the center, putting pressure on e4. We'll take on g6.} 11.hxg6 11...hxg6 {Black recaptures. Our h-file is now open.} 12.Nh3 {And not only is our development smooth, but Black's counterplay is insufficient. We're coming closer with Qe2 and Ng5, with 0-0-0 to safeguard our king. It's hard to suggest moves for Black, since their development is cramped.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 1: Black Plays 1...Nf6 and 2...c5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/06113b9d-30c7-4ee2-bc8c-5028feae3b1a/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black plays their hyper-modern move. Our bishop comes out. This is the London!} 2.Bf4 2...c5 {...c5 immediately undermines our center. In order to avoid that attack, we'll push our pawn.} 3.d5 3...b5 {Black plays in the style of the Benko Gambit! We'll play f3. Our idea is to build a strong center.} 4.f3 4...d6 {Fixing our pawn on d5. We'll build our center with e4.} 5.e4 5...b4 {Black continues expanding on the queenside. We'll play to undermine them with a3.} 6.a3 6...a5 {...a5 adds another supporter, yet it's pinned, so we'll take on b4.} 7.axb4 7...cxb4 {Black recaptures with the c-pawn. Taking with the a-pawn would have hung the rook!} 8.c3 {And we play another move to undermine the overextended queenside. If ...bxc3, we recapture with the knight and we've got Bb5+ ideas to develop with tempi on the Black pieces. After we get the bishop out, Nge2 will become possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 2: Random Moves After 1...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/7adc9b95-883a-437f-8c04-9236fa333ed0/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight in a hyper-modern style. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion. And here's where Black starts to play unusually...} 2.Bf4 2...d6 {Aiming directly for a future ...e5 is another possibility. I suggest developing the knight quickly.} 3.Nc3 3...Nbd7 {Black may insist on going for ...e5. We'll discourage it by developing our knight to f3.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {Black undermines our d4 pawn. But as is the case in many of these openings, sometimes we can just push the pawn and have more space. e4 is coming to solidify our center.} 5.d5 5...b5 {Some players with Black like to push ...b5, thinking this pawn is always immune. But we can just take it. It's not the Benko. Sometimes the pawn is just free.} 6.Nxb5 6...Qa5+ {Check! Forking our pieces. But this doesn't work. We can just retreat the knight to c3.} 7.Nc3 7...Rb8 {The rook may come to the semiopen file to attack the b-pawn. This is standard Benko-like compensation. But we can unpin the knight, inviting ...Rxb2 stuff.} 8.Bd2 8...Rxb2 {If Black takes the pawn... Our center is enviable and our development flows smoothly.} 9.e4 9...g6 {Black will fianchetto their bishop. Where else could they go? However the light-squared bishop is still on c8. We can attack the rook and form a battery, doing everything we can with tempi like we should in an opening.} 10.Qc1 10...Rb8 {The rook may retreat. This is logical.} 11.a4 {And now we play a4 to secure our a2 weakness, and we're playing Be2 next to castle! We have superior space, and a timely knight move to attack the queen. After castling, we'll play Re1 and the e5 pawn break if allowed.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 2: Random Moves After 1...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/7adc9b95-883a-437f-8c04-9236fa333ed0/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight in a hyper-modern style. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion. And here's where Black starts to play unusually...} 2.Bf4 2...c6 {Now if Black goes ...d5 we'll transpose into a 1...d5 London. We play our standard e3.} 3.e3 3...d6 {This, instead of ...d5, is a different setup we haven't seen. But it isn't particularly ambitious. We'll develop our knight with Nd2.} 4.Nd2 4...Nbd7 {Black develops as well. The idea of d6 might as well be to play ...e5 at some point. Let's fight for control of e5, and continue to develop, by playing Nf3! If we work against Black's plan, we can make their setup backfire against them.} 5.Ngf3 5...g6 {Black will fianchetto, since the e7 and d6 pawns stand in the way of the bishop. We create a retreat square for our bishop: it controls the e5-square and helps prevent the ...e5 push. In the opening, we have to take care of our best pieces!} 6.h3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Let's go into our safe home, taking care of our bishop overseeing the e5 square.} 7.Bh2 7...O-O {Black castles. We play Be2. There is little point going to d3 after g6.} 8.Be2 8...Re8 {Black adds another supporter of e5. We'll castle.} 9.O-O 9...e5 {...e5 at last. But the pawn break isn't truly effective, considering our e3-d4 pawn structure is solid.} 10.Nc4 {We can attack d6 and add an attacker on e5! Black may try an ...e4 push followed by ...d5 to defend the attacked pawns, but our knight will be able to go to d6 either way, defended by our hidden bishop on h2. White has a slight edge.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 2: Random Moves After 1...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/7adc9b95-883a-437f-8c04-9236fa333ed0/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight in a hyper-modern style. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion. And here's where Black starts to play unusually...} 2.Bf4 2...Nc6 {This is another option. But after the most played ...d6, it'll just transpose into our early Nc6 chapter. We continue normally and play e3.} 3.e3 3...d6 {So maybe ...d6 to play e5 is the only unique thing they could throw at you. But we'll develop our knight and control e5. If the entire opening revolves around ...e5 and we control it, Black is in trouble.} 4.Nf3 4...Bg4 {Black pins us. This is the only chance they may have to play ...e5. Yet we can unpin, and we'll invite ...Bxf3, giving us the bishop pair, in exchange for allowing ...e5.} 5.Be2 5...Bxf3 {Taking the knight to play e5 is futile... After we recapture with the bishop, e5 would be a serious blunder given that we can take the knight on c6 with the bishop and simply win a pawn.} 6.Bxf3 6...e6 {That's why ...e6 would be the sound move.} 7.O-O {But after castling, we have the bishop pair and Black hasn't decided how they want to play in the center. We can continue with c4 and Nc3, developing our pieces. Our bishops will prove advantageous in the long run.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 2: Random Moves After 1...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/7adc9b95-883a-437f-8c04-9236fa333ed0/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight in a hyper-modern style. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion. And here's where Black starts to play unusually...} 2.Bf4 2...b6 {Black will fianchetto. Ok, this might seem like something different, but if you play your standard London moves, you may notice Black may veer into something more familiar. We'll continue with our usual e3.} 3.e3 3...Bb7 {Black will fianchetto. And after Nf3, should they go e6, we'll have transposed into the Nf6/e6 chapter. Remember that in that chapter you developed with Nc3 and jumping to b5.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {If d5, or something entirely different (there are many possibilities not covered).} 5.c3 {Just remember to play the standard London moves you already know. You'll enjoy a nice advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: Other Nf6 Lines / Study 2: Random Moves After 1...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/ecf8bc19-ba19-42dd-8917-498af818920a/studies/7adc9b95-883a-437f-8c04-9236fa333ed0/lines"]

1.d4 1...Nf6 {Black develops their knight in a hyper-modern style. Our bishop comes out in standard London fashion. And here's where Black starts to play unusually...} 2.Bf4 2...c6 {Now if Black goes ...d5 we'll transpose into a 1...d5 London. We play our standard e3.} 3.e3 3...Qb6 {Black can introduce this move. But we push our b-pawn and defend our b2 weakness.} 4.b3 4...d6 {Black may yet again try for ...e5. We'll cover the g4 square and prevent Black from pinning our knight when we bring it to f3.} 5.h3 5...g6 {Black will fianchetto their bishop. What else? We'll develop our king's knight, adding further control to the e5 square.} 6.Nf3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes.} 7.c3 {And now we achieve our standard London pawn structure and block the bishop's diagonal. We'll continue with Nbd2 (with Nc4 as a threat) and Qc2. Instead of Bd3 like in the London, we'll go Be2, since aiming at g6 with our bishop doesn't make much sense.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The London Anti-Dutch / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/71092726-17d8-4b4a-a18f-6ee8a0deab1c/studies/42e4459c-4656-463d-bd7c-e164cec15cfe/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 3.e3 3...e6 4.Nf3 4...Be7 5.Bd3 5...O-O 6.Nbd2 6...d6 7.Qe2 7...Nc6 8.c3 8...Qe8 9.O-O-O 9...Bd8 10.e4 10...e5 11.dxe5 11...dxe5 12.Bg5 *

[Event "Chapter 10: The London Anti-Dutch / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/71092726-17d8-4b4a-a18f-6ee8a0deab1c/studies/42e4459c-4656-463d-bd7c-e164cec15cfe/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 3.e3 3...e6 4.Nf3 4...Be7 5.Bd3 5...O-O 6.Nbd2 6...b6 7.c3 7...Bb7 8.h4 8...d6 9.Qe2 9...Ne4 10.O-O-O 10...Nd7 11.Rhg1 *

[Event "Chapter 10: The London Anti-Dutch / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/71092726-17d8-4b4a-a18f-6ee8a0deab1c/studies/42e4459c-4656-463d-bd7c-e164cec15cfe/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 3.e3 3...e6 4.Nf3 4...Be7 5.Bd3 5...O-O 6.Nbd2 6...c5 7.c3 7...Qb6 8.Nc4 8...Qc6 9.O-O *

[Event "Chapter 10: The London Anti-Dutch / Study 2: Black Plays 3...g6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/71092726-17d8-4b4a-a18f-6ee8a0deab1c/studies/5dd41553-16a4-4661-99b3-578bc1a9c2b9/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {Black pushes and controls e4. We'll go for our London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We connect our pawns with the traditional e3.} 3.e3 3...g6 {20% of games, more than 300,000, go like this. If we let them, Black will go for the moves and plans that they're used to. But because we don't yet have a knight on f3, we can push the h-pawn, with the threat of h5. Black has to be really careful now.} 4.h4 4...Bg7 {The most common move is the mechanical 4...Bg7. Black fianchettoes out of inertia. Nearly 70% of games go like this. We've already played h4, hence we don't want to waste time with moves which may not be directly relevant to the current position. We'll play Nc3!} 5.Nc3 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll push forward, playing a classic breakthrough with h5. The idea is to open up the h-file with an exchange sacrifice.} 6.h5 6...Nxh5 {The knight or pawn may take, it makes little difference. 6...gxh5 allows us 7.Nge2 and Black's structure has been wrecked. In this variation, at least Black gets a little material. Still, we have a great attack.} 7.Rxh5 7...gxh5 {The pawn recaptures. Our queen will be very active on h5.} 8.Qxh5 8...Qe8 {Black may be desperate for a queen trade. Normally this makes sense for the defending side, but here we have a neat tactical follow-up which means we're simply winning after the queens come off.} 9.Qxe8 9...Rxe8 {Black recaptures. But after Nd5, c7 is a weakness that cannot be defended. Note how little development Black has, and the row of unmoved pawns.} 10.Nd5 10...Na6 {Black is lost.} 11.Bxa6 {It should be a trivial win now. I feel that our clever 4.h4 will take your opponent into unknown territory and you'll score many wins like this.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The London Anti-Dutch / Study 2: Black Plays 3...g6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/71092726-17d8-4b4a-a18f-6ee8a0deab1c/studies/5dd41553-16a4-4661-99b3-578bc1a9c2b9/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {Black pushes and controls e4. We'll go for our London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We connect our pawns with the traditional e3.} 3.e3 3...g6 {20% of games, more than 300,000, go like this. If we let them, Black will go for the moves and plans that they're used to. But because we don't yet have a knight on f3, we can push the h-pawn, with the threat of h5. Black has to be really careful now.} 4.h4 4...Bg7 {The most common move is the mechanical 4...Bg7. Black fianchettoes out of inertia. Nearly 70% of games go like this. We've already played h4, hence we don't want to waste time with moves which may not be directly relevant to the current position. We'll play Nc3!} 5.Nc3 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll push forward, playing a classic breakthrough with h5. The idea is to open up the h-file with an exchange sacrifice.} 6.h5 6...Nxh5 {The knight or pawn may take, it makes little difference. 6...gxh5 allows us 7.Nge2 and Black's structure has been wrecked. In this variation, at least Black gets a little material. Still, we have a great attack.} 7.Rxh5 7...gxh5 {The pawn recaptures. Our queen will be very active on h5.} 8.Qxh5 8...d6 {This natural move is a huge mistake. Bc4 arrives with check! One of our pieces comes alive with a tempo.} 9.Bc4+ 9...e6 {The pawn blocks. After Nf3, Black is in great trouble. All of our pieces are coming together in the attack.} 10.Nf3 10...Qe8 {If they beg for a queen trade, we don't have to accept it here. Since keeping the pressure is better, we'll move our queen away. h2 is a great square for her, keeping an eye on e5.} 11.Qh2 11...Qg6 {Black brings their queen to g6 to try and defend their king.} 12.O-O-O {The engine gives us a +2 advantage because of our activity. We have more than enough compensation for the exchange.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The London Anti-Dutch / Study 2: Black Plays 3...g6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6f04b1ed-6baf-48cd-9087-5b1d3b40fcbf/chapters/71092726-17d8-4b4a-a18f-6ee8a0deab1c/studies/5dd41553-16a4-4661-99b3-578bc1a9c2b9/lines"]

1.d4 1...f5 {Black pushes and controls e4. We'll go for our London.} 2.Bf4 2...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We connect our pawns with the traditional e3.} 3.e3 3...g6 {20% of games, more than 300,000, go like this. If we let them, Black will go for the moves and plans that they're used to. But because we don't yet have a knight on f3, we can push the h-pawn, with the threat of h5. Black has to be really careful now.} 4.h4 4...h6 {Some people will try to keep the kingside solid, intending to reply to h5 with ...g5. But we have other avenues of attack. Black's move has weakened g6, and with this in mind, we'll bring out our queen to f3.} 5.Qf3 5...Bg7 {Many people don't see the threat... which is to fork g6 and c7 by playing Qg3!} 6.Qg3 6...Kf7 {Black can defend one pawn.} 7.Bxc7 {But they can't defend both. This is just proof of how our London System can beat Black's Dutch to the punch.} *